      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "pthm-gr-schwarzschild-vacuum-model",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Adopt the postulates [[post-gr-spacetime-and-classical-domain]],\n[[post-gr-field-and-matter-assumptions]],\n[[post-gr-einstein-field-law]] and\n[[post-gr-ideal-clocks-and-free-fall]] of the classical gravitational\nframework, with the field law in its direct-equation presentation and the unit\nconventions of [[def-gr-si-unit-and-coordinate-conventions]]. Let\n$(M,g,N)$ be the Schwarzschild exterior datum of mass parameter $m_*>0$ with\nSchwarzschild radius $s=2G_Nm_*/c^2$ and static congruence\n$n=f^{-1/2}\\partial_0$, $f(r)=1-s/r$\n([[def-gr-schwarzschild-exterior-model]]), and let the model's stress-energy\ntensor be the vacuum prescription\n\n$$T:=0\\qquad\\text{on }M,\\qquad \\Lambda:=0 .$$\n\nThen all statements below are conditional deductions inside the model so\nspecified; they assert nothing about nature.\n\n**(a) The Schwarzschild vacuum model.** $(M,g,N)$ satisfies the spacetime\npostulate, $T=0$ is a smooth symmetric stress-energy tensor field on the\nmatter domain $M$ in the sense of [[def-gr-stress-energy-tensor-field]], the\nprescription is admissible, and the adopted field law\n$G_{ab}+\\Lambda g_{ab}=\\kappa T_{ab}$ holds identically on $M$. A **vacuum solution with zero cosmological parameter** here means\nprecisely a datum satisfying that equation with $T=0$ and $\\Lambda=0$;\nthis datum is such a solution, read as a model of the framework. The model is not locally flat: its curvature tensor is nowhere\nzero, while its Ricci tensor and Einstein tensor vanish identically\n([[lem-gr-schwarzschild-ricci-and-einstein-tensors]] clause (d)).\n\n**(b) Static clock readings.** The integral curves of $n$ are the static\nobserver worldlines at constant radius $r_0$\n([[lem-gr-schwarzschild-static-congruence-and-clock-rates]] clause (b));\nadopting the clock rule of [[post-gr-ideal-clocks-and-free-fall]] clause (i),\nthe reading difference of an ideal clock carried along such a worldline\nbetween two events whose coordinate times differ by $\\Delta t$ is\n\n$$\\Delta\\tau=\\sqrt{f(r_0)}\\,\\Delta t=\\sqrt{1-\\frac{s}{r_0}}\\;\\Delta t,$$\n\nso two static clocks at radii $r_1,r_2$ compared over the same coordinate\ninterval with $\\Delta t\\ne0$ satisfy $\\Delta\\tau_1/\\Delta\\tau_2=\\sqrt{f(r_1)/f(r_2)}$. The static\nworldlines are not geodesics: their acceleration is\n$a=\\frac{s}{2r^2}\\partial_r\\ne0$, so the model assigns them to accelerated\n(constrained) bodies and not to freely falling ones.\n\n**(c) The mass parameter.** The parameter $m_*$ enters the model only through\n$s=2G_Nm_*/c^2$ and through the quantities below; the framework reads $m_*$ as\nthe mass parameter of the model in kilograms\n([[def-gr-si-unit-and-coordinate-conventions]] clause (a),\n[[def-gr-schwarzschild-exterior-model]] clause (a)):\n\n1. Let $\\gamma:I\\to M$ be a smooth future timelike geodesic, parametrized\n   by proper time $\\tau$ on an interval with nonempty interior, with\n   $r\\circ\\gamma\\equiv r_0>\\frac{3s}{2}$. Put $\\sigma:=\\iota\\circ\\gamma:I\\to S^2$\n   and $t:=(x^0\\circ\\gamma)/c$. Its **angular speed with respect to $t$** is\n   $\\Omega:=\\sqrt{\\gamma_{S^2}(d\\sigma/dt,d\\sigma/dt)}$, where\n   $d\\sigma/dt:=\\sigma'(\\tau)/t'(\\tau)$ and $t'(\\tau)>0$ by future\n   timelikeness. It has units $\\mathrm s^{-1}$ and satisfies\n   $\\Omega^2=G_Nm_*/r_0^3$ in every angular orientation. For an equatorial\n   curve, $\\vartheta=\\pi/2$ and $\\Omega^2=(d\\varphi/dt)^2$ in local polar\n   charts ([[lem-gr-schwarzschild-circular-orbits-and-photon-sphere]]\n   clause (a) and proof step 2.2). This is the Kepler-like law in which\n   $m_*$ plays the role of the central mass;\n2. in the isotropic chart of\n   [[lem-gr-schwarzschild-isotropic-coordinate-form]], with\n   $a=s/(4\\rho)$, the exact identity\n   $g_{00}+1=1-\\bigl((1-a)/(1+a)\\bigr)^2=4a/(1+a)^2$ gives\n   $$g_{00}+1=+\\frac{s}{\\rho}+O(\\rho^{-2})=+\\frac{2G_Nm_*}{c^2\\rho}+O(\\rho^{-2}).$$\n   Under the weak-field convention $g_{00}=-(1+2\\Phi/c^2)$ to leading order\n   ([[def-gr-stationary-weak-field-data]]), this is the coefficient\n   $-2\\Phi_N/c^2$ for the explicit exterior Newtonian potential\n   $\\Phi_N(\\rho):=-G_Nm_*/\\rho$ in the comparison reading of\n   [[def-gr-newtonian-comparison-model-and-identification]].\n\nThe reading is a statement about the model's own identities and the adopted\nunit and weak-field conventions; no numerical value of $G_N$ or $m_*$, no\nNewtonian source model realized by matter and no comparison with any reported\nmass is asserted.\n\n**(d) Scope.** The model is an idealized vacuum model of the framework, not a\nclaim about the actual universe: no observation, apparatus, calibration or\nuncertainty is used, and no comparison with data is made. The datum solves the\nfield law with the chosen parameters $(m_*,\\Lambda,T)=(m_*,0,0)$; other\nsolutions of the same field law exist and are not excluded, and the exterior\ndomain $r>s$ contains no horizon or interior region, whose treatment is the\nsubject of the extension and Kruskal items of this page. The physical\ninterpretation of coordinates, clocks and bodies remains the adopted one of\nthe cited postulates.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "post-gr-ideal-clocks-and-free-fall",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Adopt as physical assumptions of the classical gravitational framework, in\naddition to [[post-gr-spacetime-and-classical-domain]] and the matter-content\nassumptions of the framework, the following rules by which ideal clocks, test\nbodies and light couple to the metric $g$ of\n[[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]].\n\n**(i) Ideal clocks.** An **ideal clock** is an idealized physical device\ncarried along a future timelike worldline ([[def-gr-timelike-worldline-and-proper-time]])\nwhose readings are assumed to reproduce the proper time of that worldline:\nthe difference of two readings taken at two events of the worldline is the\nproper-time increment between those events as defined by the metric, and it is\nindependent of the construction of the clock. Two ideal clocks moving along\nthe same worldline differ only by an additive constant (their choice of zero);\ndifferent worldlines generally accrue different proper times between the same\npair of events. The physical constant $c$ and the unit of duration are those\nof [[def-gr-si-unit-and-coordinate-conventions]].\n\n**(ii) Free fall of massive test bodies.** A **test body** is an uncharged\nidealized body whose internal structure is small compared with the scale over\nwhich the geometry of the model varies and whose gravitational self-field is\nnegligible within the model. An **ideal freely falling massive test body** is\nassumed to have a worldline that is a future timelike geodesic of the\nmetric-selected connection ([[def-gr-metric-selected-connection]],\n[[thm-gr-nondegenerate-levi-civita-and-tensor-derivative]],\n[[def-geodesic-of-an-affine-connection]]), traversed with physical\nfour-velocity $U=d\\gamma/d\\tau$ satisfying\n$g_{\\gamma(\\tau)}(U,U)=-c^2$; proper time is an affine parameter of the\nworldline ([[lem-gr-proper-time-reparametrization-and-four-velocity]]). The\nworldline is determined by the event at which the body is released together\nwith its four-velocity there, by the uniqueness of the geodesic initial-value\nproblem ([[thm-gr-local-geodesics-and-exponential-map]]); no force, charge, or\nrest mass of the body enters this rule.\n\n**(iii) Light rays.** In the geometric-optics approximation an **ideal light\nray** propagating in the model is assumed to be a future null geodesic of the\nmetric-selected connection\n([[def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors]],\n[[def-geodesic-of-an-affine-connection]]). A null worldline carries no proper\ntime and no four-velocity in the sense of\n[[def-gr-timelike-worldline-and-proper-time]]; within the approximation the\npropagation of an ideal ray is independent of its frequency, and only the\nray's direction in the tangent space is fixed, not a scale along it.\n\n**(iv) Scope and exclusions.** The three rules are adopted in the test-field\nregime of the smooth classical model: the clocks, bodies and rays do not\nback-react on the geometry, and the geometry varies smoothly on the scales\nrelevant to them. Configurations requiring the body's own gravitational field,\nradiation reaction, an electromagnetic or other self-force, finite-size or\ntidal couplings beyond the test-body idealization, non-geometric-optics\npropagation, dispersion or dissipation of light, or quantum effects are\noutside these assumptions and require additional model content. The\nassumptions do not assert that any real clock, body or light ray is exactly\nideal, nor do they attach measured readings to any model.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-gr-schwarzschild-timelike-geodesic-reduction",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(M,g,N)$ be the Schwarzschild exterior datum with $s>0$ and\n$f(r)=1-s/r$ ([[def-gr-schwarzschild-exterior-model]]), with\nmetric-selected connection $\\nabla$. Let $\\gamma:I\\to M$ be a **future\ntimelike geodesic**, parametrized by proper time $\\tau$, with tangent\n$U=\\gamma'$, so that $g(U,U)=-c^2$ and $U$ is future-directed\n([[def-geodesic-of-an-affine-connection]],\n[[lem-gr-parallel-transport-and-geodesic-speed]] clause 3,\n[[def-gr-timelike-worldline-and-proper-time]]). Fix a spherical-polar product chart\n$(x^0,r,\\vartheta,\\varphi)$ containing the image of $\\gamma$ and write a dot\nfor $\\frac{d}{d\\tau}$. Define\n\n$$e:=-g(U,\\partial_0)=f\\dot x^0,\\qquad \\ell:=g(U,\\partial_\\varphi)=r^2\\sin^2\\vartheta\\,\\dot\\varphi .$$\n\nThen:\n\n**(a) Conserved quantities.** $\\partial_0$ and $\\partial_\\varphi$ are Killing\nfields of $g$, and $e$ and $\\ell$ are constant along $\\gamma$; moreover\n$e>0$.\n\n**(b) Equatorial reduction.** Suppose $\\vartheta(\\tau_0)=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and\n$\\dot\\vartheta(\\tau_0)=0$ at some $\\tau_0\\in I$. Then\n$\\vartheta(\\tau)=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ for all $\\tau\\in I$; the geodesic is\n**equatorial**.\n\n**(c) Radial reduction.** If $\\gamma$ is equatorial, define the **effective\npotential**\n\n$$W_\\ell(r):=f(r)\\Bigl(c^2+\\frac{\\ell^2}{r^2}\\Bigr).$$\n\nThen the normalization identity\n\n$$\\dot r^2=e^2-W_\\ell(r)$$\n\nholds at every $\\tau\\in I$, and, given this identity, the $r$-component of the\ngeodesic equation is equivalent to the one-dimensional equation\n\n$$\\ddot r=-\\frac12 W_\\ell'(r),\\qquad W_\\ell'(r)=f'(r)\\Bigl(c^2+\\frac{\\ell^2}{r^2}\\Bigr)-2f(r)\\frac{\\ell^2}{r^3}.$$\n\n**(d) Scope.** The reduction is local: it is stated on the interval $I$ on\nwhich the geodesic stays in one product chart with $r>s$ and\n$0<\\vartheta<\\pi$; no completeness, no extension of $\\gamma$ beyond $I$, and\nno statement about null geodesics is made. The quantities $e$ and $\\ell$ are\nconstants of the given geodesic, not functions of the model.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-gr-schwarzschild-circular-orbits-and-photon-sphere",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(M,g,N)$ be the Schwarzschild exterior datum with $s>0$ and\n$f(r)=1-s/r$ ([[def-gr-schwarzschild-exterior-model]]), with\n$f'=s/r^2$. Then:\n\nFor arbitrary $x^0_0,\\varphi_0\\in\\mathbb R$, put\n$\\omega_0=(\\cos\\varphi_0,\\sin\\varphi_0,0)\\in S^2$. For $\\alpha\\in\\mathbb R$\ndefine the rotation\n$$R_\\alpha(y_1,y_2,y_3):=(y_1\\cos\\alpha-y_2\\sin\\alpha,\\ y_1\\sin\\alpha+y_2\\cos\\alpha,\\ y_3),$$\nand the global axial vector field $\\Phi(y_1,y_2,y_3):=(-y_2,y_1,0)$ on $S^2$;\nit is tangent because $y\\cdot\\Phi(y)=0$ for $y\\in S^2$.\nIn every spherical-polar chart near the equator, $\\Phi=\\partial_\\varphi$;\nlongitude expressions below are local chart expressions for the global sphere\ncurve $R_\\alpha\\omega_0$.\n\nIn this item, a **timelike circular geodesic** means a future timelike\ngeodesic whose areal radius $r$ is constant on an interval of positive length;\nits angular orientation is unrestricted.\n\n**(a) Existence of circular timelike geodesics.** Let $r_0>3s/2$ and choose\n$\\ell\\in\\mathbb R$ with\n\n$$\\ell^2=\\frac{c^2sr_0^2}{2r_0-3s}>0,\\qquad e:=\\Bigl(f(r_0)\\Bigl(c^2+\\frac{\\ell^2}{r_0^2}\\Bigr)\\Bigr)^{1/2} =\\frac{c(r_0-s)\\sqrt 2}{\\sqrt{r_0(2r_0-3s)}}>0 .$$\n\nThen the globally defined curve $\\gamma:\\mathbb R\\to M$ is\n\n$$\\gamma(\\tau):=\\bigl(x^0_0+\\tfrac{e}{f(r_0)}\\tau,\\ r_0,\\ R_{\\ell\\tau/r_0^2}\\omega_0\\bigr).$$\nIn each local polar chart along it, $\\vartheta=\\pi/2$ and the longitude is\n$\\varphi_0+\\ell\\tau/r_0^2$ up to a constant multiple of $2\\pi$.\n\nIt is a future timelike geodesic of the datum, with $g(\\gamma',\\gamma')=-c^2$\nand with the conserved quantities $e=-g(\\gamma',\\partial_0)$ and\n$\\ell=g(\\gamma',\\Phi)$ of\n[[lem-gr-schwarzschild-timelike-geodesic-reduction]]; it is a **circular\norbit** at radius $r_0$. Its angular velocity in the coordinate $x^0$ is\n\n$$\\frac{d\\varphi}{dx^0}=\\frac{\\ell f(r_0)}{r_0^2e}=\\operatorname{sgn}(\\ell)\\Bigl(\\frac{s}{2r_0^3}\\Bigr)^{1/2},\\qquad\\text{so}\\qquad \\Bigl(\\frac{d\\varphi}{dt}\\Bigr)^2=\\frac{G_Nm_*}{r_0^3},$$\n\n$t=x^0/c$.\n\n**(b) The circular-orbit condition is necessary.** Conversely, if\n$\\gamma:I\\to M$ is any future timelike geodesic parametrized by proper time with\n$r(\\tau)\\equiv r_0>s$ constant on an interval of positive length, then\n$r_0>\\frac{3s}{2}$. If $\\gamma$ is equatorial, its conserved angular\nmomentum additionally satisfies $\\ell^2=\\dfrac{c^2sr_0^2}{2r_0-3s}$.\nConsequently no timelike circular geodesic, in any angular orientation, exists\nwith $s<r_0\\le\\frac{3s}{2}$.\n\n**(c) Stability threshold of the radial potential.** For each radius\n$r>\\frac{3s}{2}$ set $\\ell_r^2:=c^2sr^2/(2r-3s)$ and regard $\\ell_r$ as a\nconstant in the function $V_{\\ell_r}(x):=\\tfrac12 f(x)(c^2+\\ell_r^2/x^2)$.\nDifferentiate with respect to $x$ at fixed $\\ell_r$, then evaluate at $x=r$.\nThe result, evaluated at the circular value $\\ell_r$, is\n\n$$V_{\\ell_r}''(r)=\\frac{c^2s(r-3s)}{r^3(2r-3s)},\\qquad r>\\frac{3s}{2},$$\n\nso $V_\\ell$ has a strict local minimum at the circular-orbit radius exactly\nfor $r>3s$, a strict local maximum exactly for $\\frac{3s}{2}<r<3s$, and\n$V_\\ell''(3s)=0$; the radius $r=3s$ is the **innermost stable circular orbit\nradius** of the restricted model in this potential sense.\n\n**(d) The photon sphere at $r=3s/2$.** Let $\\ell\\neq0$ and put\n$r_0=\\frac{3s}{2}$ and $e_0:=\\dfrac{\\lvert\\ell\\rvert\\sqrt{f(r_0)}}{r_0}=\\dfrac{2\\lvert\\ell\\rvert}{3\\sqrt3\\,s}>0$.\nThen the globally defined curve\n$$\\eta(\\tau):=\\bigl(x^0_0+\\tfrac{e_0}{f(r_0)}\\tau,\\ r_0,\\ R_{\\ell\\tau/r_0^2}\\omega_0\\bigr),\\qquad \\tau\\in\\mathbb R,$$\nwhose local polar expressions have $\\vartheta=\\pi/2$ and longitude\n$\\varphi_0+\\ell\\tau/r_0^2$ up to chart shifts, is a future null geodesic (a\n**null circular orbit** or **photon sphere**\norbit) with conserved quantities $e_0=-g(\\eta',\\partial_0)$ and\n$\\ell=g(\\eta',\\Phi)$, and its angular velocity is\n\n$$\\frac{d\\varphi}{dx^0}=\\frac{\\ell f(r_0)}{r_0^2e_0}=\\operatorname{sgn}(\\ell)\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{f(r_0)}}{r_0},\\qquad \\Bigl(\\frac{d\\varphi}{dx^0}\\Bigr)^2=\\frac{f(r_0)}{r_0^2}=\\frac{4}{27s^2}.$$\n\nThe **null effective potential** $W^{\\mathrm n}_\\ell(r):=f(r)\\ell^2/r^2$\nsatisfies $(W^{\\mathrm n}_\\ell)'(3s/2)=0$ and\n$(W^{\\mathrm n}_\\ell)''(3s/2)=-\\dfrac{32\\ell^2}{81s^4}<0$, so the photon-sphere\nradius is a strict local maximum of the null potential.\n\n**(e) Scope.** All statements are exact data of the restricted model: the\norbits are ideal geodesics of the metric on the exterior domain, the\nstability claim is the sign of the second derivative of the stated potential,\nand no claim is made about nonlinear stability, about orbital decay, about\nobservations or about the interior region. The case $r_0=s$ is excluded, and\nthe reduction is stated only where $r>s$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "2.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-cm-equilibrium-and-lyapunov-stability",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $n\\ge1$, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open and let $F:U\\to\\mathbb R^n$ be a map. Consider the autonomous first-order system\n\n$$z'(t)=F(z(t)),$$\n\nread as the first-order system of [[def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution]] with $D=\\mathbb R\\times U$ and right-hand side $(t,z)\\mapsto F(z)$. A **solution** below means a solution of this system on an interval containing $0$; a solution whose interval is $[0,\\infty)$ or contains it is said to be **defined for all $t\\ge0$**.\n\nA solution $\\tilde z:J\\to U$ **extends** $z:I\\to U$ when $I\\subseteq J$ and $\\tilde z|_I=z$; the extension is proper if $I\\subsetneq J$. A solution is **maximal** when it has no proper extension ([[def-maximal-ode-solution]]). All stability predicates below quantify over maximal solutions whose intervals contain $0$; their local restrictions remain solutions. This convention asserts neither existence nor uniqueness of maximal solutions for an arbitrary map $F$.\n\nA point $z_*\\in U$ is an **equilibrium** (also **fixed point** or **zero**) of $F$ when $F(z_*)=0$; then the constant function $z(t)=z_*$ is a solution on every interval containing $0$.\n\nLet $z_*\\in U$ be an equilibrium.\n\n1. $z_*$ is **Lyapunov stable** (or simply **stable**) when for every Euclidean ball $B_\\varepsilon(z_*)$ with $\\overline{B_\\varepsilon(z_*)}\\subseteq U$ ([[def-metric-ball]]) there is $\\delta\\in(0,\\varepsilon]$ such that every maximal solution $z$ with $\\|z(0)-z_*\\|_2<\\delta$ ([[def-euclidean-inner-product]]) is defined for all $t\\ge0$ and satisfies\n\n$$\\|z(t)-z_*\\|_2<\\varepsilon\\qquad(t\\ge0).$$\n\n2. $z_*$ is **asymptotically stable** when it is Lyapunov stable and in addition there is $\\delta_0>0$ such that every maximal solution $z$ with $\\|z(0)-z_*\\|_2<\\delta_0$ is defined for all $t\\ge0$ and satisfies $\\|z(t)-z_*\\|_2\\to0$ as $t\\to\\infty$.\n\nAn equilibrium which is not Lyapunov stable is **unstable**. An equilibrium is **globally asymptotically stable** when it is asymptotically stable and every maximal solution of the system is defined for all $t\\ge0$ and converges to it as $t\\to\\infty$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "2.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-picard-lindelof-local-existence-and-uniqueness",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $D\\subseteq\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^n$ be open, let $F:D\\to\\mathbb R^n$ be continuous and locally Lipschitz in the state variable, and let $(t_0,x_0)\\in D$. Then a unique local solution of the IVP exists on some interval around the initial time. More quantitatively, if $h,r>0$ make\n\n$$[t_0-h,t_0+h]\\times\\overline B(x_0,r)\\subseteq D,$$\n\nif $\\|F\\|_2\\le M$ and $F$ has state-Lipschitz constant $L$ on this cylinder, and if $hM\\le r$ and $Lh<1$, then there is exactly one solution on $[t_0-h,t_0+h]$ through $(t_0,x_0)$ whose graph lies in the cylinder. Any two solutions through the same initial data agree on every common subinterval containing $t_0$.\n\nIn particular, a unique local solution exists on an interval around the initial time.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "2.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-cm-compact-continuation-for-locally-lipschitz-odes",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n\\ge1$, let $D\\subseteq\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^n$ be open, and let $F:D\\to\\mathbb R^n$ be continuous and locally Lipschitz in the state variable, locally uniformly in time, in the sense of [[def-locally-lipschitz-in-the-state-variable]]. Let $t_0<b$ be reals, let $x_0\\in\\mathbb R^n$ with $(t_0,x_0)\\in D$, and let $x:[t_0,b)\\to\\mathbb R^n$ be a solution of the initial value problem\n\n$$x'(t)=F\\bigl(t,x(t)\\bigr),\\qquad x(t_0)=x_0$$\n\non $[t_0,b)$ ([[def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution]]). Suppose there is a compact set $K\\subseteq D$ and a real $\\delta>0$ such that\n\n$$\\bigl(t,x(t)\\bigr)\\in K\\qquad\\text{for every }t\\in(b-\\delta,b)\\cap[t_0,b).$$\n\nThen:\n\n1. there is $\\varepsilon>0$ and a solution $\\tilde x:[t_0,b+\\varepsilon)\\to\\mathbb R^n$ of the same initial value problem with $\\tilde x(t)=x(t)$ for all $t\\in[t_0,b)$; in particular $\\tilde x(b)=\\lim_{t\\uparrow b}x(t)$ exists and lies in the state projection of $K$;\n2. if moreover $D=\\mathbb R\\times U$ for an open $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ and $F$ is autonomous, $F(t,z)=G(z)$ with $G:U\\to\\mathbb R^n$ continuous and locally Lipschitz in the state variable ([[def-locally-lipschitz-in-the-state-variable]]), and if there is a compact $K_0\\subseteq U$ with $x(t)\\in K_0$ for every $t\\in(b-\\delta,b)\\cap[t_0,b)$, then the extension conclusion of clause 1 holds as well.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "By [F1], the reduced vector field is $F_\\ell(r,w)=(w,-W_\\ell'(r)/2)$ on the open set $(s,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R$. It is smooth there and [F4] gives a unique local solution through every state. Differentiating $W_\\ell=f(c^2+\\ell^2/r^2)$ gives $W_\\ell'=c^2s/r^2-2\\ell^2/r^3+3s\\ell^2/r^4$; substituting the circular value of $\\ell^2$ from [F2] yields $W_\\ell'(r_c)=0$, so $F_\\ell(r_c,0)=0$. Along every solution, $H_\\ell(r,w):=\\tfrac12(w^2+W_\\ell(r))$ satisfies $dH_\\ell/d\\tau=w(-W_\\ell'/2)+(W_\\ell'/2)w=0$. Thus $H_\\ell$ is constant on each solution interval.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Suppose $r_c>3s$. By [F2], $W_\\ell''(r_c)>0$, so continuity gives an interval about $r_c$ on which $W_\\ell''>0$. Since $W_\\ell'(r_c)=0$, the derivative is negative to the left and positive to the right there; hence $W_\\ell$ has a strict local minimum. Fix any phase-space radius $\\varepsilon>0$ whose closed ball about $z_c$ lies in $(s,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R$. Choose $0<\\eta<\\varepsilon/2$ so $[r_c-\\eta, r_c+\\eta]\\subset(s,\\infty)$ and both boundary values of $W_\\ell$ exceed $W_\\ell(r_c)$. By continuity of $H_\\ell$, choose $0<\\delta\\le\\varepsilon$ so every state within $\\delta$ of $z_c$ has $|r-r_c|<\\eta/2$, energy below both boundary potential halves, and $H_\\ell-W_\\ell(r_c)/2<\\varepsilon^2/8$. Conservation in 1.1 prevents such a solution from reaching either radial boundary, since there $H_\\ell\\ge W_\\ell(r_c\\pm\\eta)/2$. Inside the resulting radial interval $W_\\ell(r)\\ge W_\\ell(r_c)$, so $w^2/2=H_\\ell-W_\\ell(r)/2<\\varepsilon^2/8$ and $|w|<\\varepsilon/2$. The state stays in a compact subset of the open domain; [F5] rules out a finite forward maximal endpoint. With $|r-r_c|<\\eta<\\varepsilon/2$ and $|w|<\\varepsilon/2$, it remains inside the prescribed phase-space ball for every $\\tau\\ge0$. This is Lyapunov stability by [F3].",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "Suppose $3s/2<r_c<3s$. By [F2], $W_\\ell''(r_c)<0$; shrink $\\eta>0$ so $W_\\ell'(r)<0$ on $(r_c,r_c+\\eta]$. For any proposed initial-state radius $\\delta>0$, choose $r_0\\in(r_c,r_c+\\min\\{\\eta/2,\\delta/2\\})$ and initial data $(r_0,0)$. [F4] supplies a local solution. Its acceleration is $-W_\\ell'(r_0)/2>0$, and conservation of $H_\\ell$ gives, on the outgoing branch, $w=\\sqrt{W_\\ell(r_0)-W_\\ell(r)}$. The solution reaches $r_c+\\eta$ in finite time: the travel-time integral is $\\int_{r_0}^{r_c+\\eta}[W_\\ell(r_0)-W_\\ell(r)]^{-1/2}dr$, whose only endpoint singularity is bounded by a constant times $(r-r_0)^{-1/2}$ because $W_\\ell'(r_0)<0$. Hence initial data arbitrarily close to $z_c$ leave the fixed neighborhood of radius $\\eta$, contradicting [F3].",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-3",
          "claim": "At $r_c=3s$, the circular relation gives $\\ell^2=3c^2s^2$. Differentiating $W_\\ell(r)=c^2(1-s/r)+\\ell^2(r^{-2}-sr^{-3})$ at this fixed $\\ell$ gives $W_\\ell'(r)=c^2s(r-3s)^2/r^4$ and $W_\\ell''(r)=-2c^2s(r-3s)(r-6s)/r^5$. Thus $W_\\ell'$ is positive except at $3s$ and $W_\\ell''$ changes sign there, so the equilibrium is a stationary inflection. Fix $0<\\eta<s$ and, for any $\\delta>0$, choose $r_0\\in(3s-\\min\\{\\eta/2,\\delta/2\\},3s)$ with initial data $(r_0,0)$. [F4] supplies a local solution. The acceleration is negative. On the inward branch energy conservation yields $w=-\\sqrt{W_\\ell(r_0)-W_\\ell(r)}$ for $3s-\\eta\\le r<r_0$, since $W_\\ell$ is strictly increasing on that interval. The travel-time integral to $3s-\\eta$ is finite; near $r_0$ its integrand is bounded by a constant times $(r_0-r)^{-1/2}$ because $W_\\ell'(r_0)>0$. Thus arbitrarily close initial data leave the fixed neighborhood of radius $\\eta$, so [F3] says the equilibrium is not Lyapunov stable.",
          "step": "2.3",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "The two subcritical cases in 2.2–2.3 are not Lyapunov stable, while the supercritical case in 2.1 is Lyapunov stable. These are deductions under the adopted Schwarzschild free-fall model [A1] and concern only its fixed-angular-momentum radial subsystem.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1",
            "step 2.2",
            "step 2.3",
            "A1",
            "given"
          ]
        }
      ],
