    "lem-rpm-schwarzschild-null-geodesic-reduction": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-gr-schwarzschild-exterior-model",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "This item assumes the axiom of countable choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ through\nthe tangent-bundle structure used by\n[[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]] clause (c)\n([[def-countable-choice]]); no further choice principle is used.\n\n**(a) Parameters and the Schwarzschild radius.** Let $c>0$ and $G_N>0$ be real\nconstants and let $m_*>0$ be a real **mass parameter**. Put\n\n$$s:=\\frac{2G_Nm_*}{c^2}>0 .$$\n\nThe number $s$ is the **Schwarzschild radius** of the datum. Neither $c$,\n$G_N$ nor $m_*$ is measured here; the physical reading of $c$ and $G_N$ as the\nframework's conversion and Newton constants and of $m_*$ as a mass is fixed by\nthe physical conventions of the framework, not by this definition.\n\n**(b) The underlying manifold and its product charts.** Let $S^2:=S_2(0,1)=\n\\{y\\in\\mathbb R^3:\\lVert y\\rVert_2=1\\}$ be the unit Euclidean sphere with the\nsubspace topology of [[def-euclidean-spheres-and-closed-balls]]. Specify its\nsmooth structure as the maximal atlas generated by the following two charts\n([[def-smooth-manifold]]). For $\\varepsilon\\in\\{+1,-1\\}$ put\n$p_\\varepsilon=(0,0,\\varepsilon)$ and\n$U_\\varepsilon=S^2\\setminus\\{p_\\varepsilon\\}$, and define\n\n$$\\sigma_\\varepsilon:U_\\varepsilon\\to\\mathbb R^2,\\qquad \\sigma_\\varepsilon(y)=\\frac{(y_1,y_2)}{1-\\varepsilon y_3}.$$\n\nThese open domains cover $S^2$. Each chart is a homeomorphism onto\n$\\mathbb R^2$, with inverse, for $u=(u_1,u_2)$ and $q=u_1^2+u_2^2$,\n\n$$\\sigma_\\varepsilon^{-1}(u)=\\left(\\frac{2u_1}{1+q},\\frac{2u_2}{1+q},\\varepsilon\\frac{q-1}{1+q}\\right).$$\n\nThe two inverse identities follow by substitution; all displayed denominators\nare nonzero on their domains. On the overlap both transition maps are\n$u\\mapsto u/(u_1^2+u_2^2)$ on $\\mathbb R^2\\setminus\\{0\\}$, hence smooth.\nThe subspace is Hausdorff and has a countable basis given by intersections\nwith Euclidean balls of rational centres and positive rational radii.\nThus these charts specify a smooth two-manifold without boundary. This is\nthe standard sphere structure used for the round metric below: the inclusion\nhas the displayed smooth local parametrizations, and their differentials\nare injective because composition with $\\sigma_\\varepsilon$ is the identity.\nLet $M$ be the product manifold\n\n$$M:=\\mathbb R\\times(s,\\infty)\\times S^2$$\n\nwith the product smooth structure\n([[prop-products-of-smooth-manifolds-have-a-canonical-product-smooth-structure]]);\nit is a smooth four-manifold without boundary. Write $x^0:M\\to\\mathbb R$ and\n$r:M\\to(s,\\infty)$ for the first and second projections and $\\iota:M\\to S^2$\nfor the third. A **product chart** of $M$ is a smooth chart on the product\nwhose angular part is a smooth chart of $S^2$. In a **spherical-polar product\nchart**, $0<\\vartheta<\\pi$ and the components of the round metric\n$\\gamma_{S^2}$ of [[ex-the-round-metric-on-the-sphere-as-an-induced-metric]]\nare $\\gamma_{22}=1$, $\\gamma_{23}=\\gamma_{32}=0$,\n$\\gamma_{33}=\\sin^2\\vartheta$. These polar charts omit the two poles; ordinary\nsmooth sphere charts around the poles complete the product atlas.\n\n**(c) The metric.** Let $f:(s,\\infty)\\to(0,\\infty)$ be the smooth function\n\n$$f(r):=1-\\frac{s}{r} .$$\n\nDefine $g$ globally by the coordinate-free expression\n\n$$g=-f\\,d(x^0)\\otimes d(x^0)+f^{-1}\\,dr\\otimes dr+r^2\\,\\iota^*\\gamma_{S^2}.$$\n\nIn every spherical-polar product chart its component functions are\n\n$$g_{00}:=-f(r),\\qquad g_{11}:=\\frac{1}{f(r)},\\qquad g_{22}:=r^2,\\qquad g_{33}:=r^2\\sin^2\\vartheta,$$\n\nand $g_{ab}:=0$ whenever $a\\ne b$, $a,b\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}$, with the index order\n$(x^0,x^1,x^2,x^3)=(x^0,r,\\vartheta,\\varphi)$. This is a smooth symmetric\ncovariant $2$-tensor field on all of $M$\n([[def-gr-smooth-covariant-two-tensor-field]]); the component display is local\nto spherical-polar charts, and $f>0$ because $r>s$. The pair $(M,g)$ is the **Schwarzschild exterior\nmetric of mass parameter $m_*$**; the datum of this definition is the\nquadruple $(c,G_N,m_*,g)$. With these parameters fixed, write $(M,g,N)$\nfor its associated geometric datum, with $N$ as in clause (d). The geometric\ndatum depends on the parameters only through $s$ and does not retain $c$,\n$G_N$ and $m_*$ separately. That $(M,g,N)$ is a time-oriented smooth Lorentzian four-manifold\nof signature $(-,+,+,+)$ is not asserted here; it is proved in the companion metric-properties lemma of this page (its\njustification record in the frontmatter), which also records the equivalent\ncoordinate-free description\n$g=-f\\,d(x^0)\\otimes d(x^0)+f^{-1}\\,dr\\otimes dr+r^2\\,\\iota^*\\gamma_{S^2}$.\n\n**(d) Time orientation and the Schwarzschild chart.** The field\n$N:=\\partial_0$ is the smooth product vector field on $M$\n([[def-derivation-at-a-point-and-tangent-space]]); the chart components of $g$\nare independent of $x^0$, so $N$ is the restriction of a global vector field,\nand $g(N,N)=-f(r)<0$ at every point. The datum carries the time orientation\n$N$. Spherical-polar product charts are **Schwarzschild charts** of the datum;\nat the poles the same metric is represented in the smooth sphere charts of\nthe product atlas. We write $t$ for the coordinate $x^0/c$ when the datum is\nread in the framework's unit conventions.\n\n**(e) Scope.** The domain of the datum is the exterior region $r>s$; the\ndefinition asserts nothing about $r=s$, about $r\\le 0$, about any matter, or\nabout any equation satisfied by $g$. The horizon $r=s$ is not part of the\ndatum, and a continuation of the metric to $r=s$ requires the separate constructions recorded on this page, the\nregularity lemma for the advanced chart and the Kruskal model-properties\nlemma. Whether the datum is a solution of the\nframework's field law is proved, not defined, in the Ricci-and-Einstein-tensor lemma of this\npage.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-gr-schwarzschild-metric-properties",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $c>0$, $G_N>0$, $m_*>0$ and let $(M,g,N)$ be the Schwarzschild exterior\ndatum of mass parameter $m_*$ with Schwarzschild radius\n$s=2G_Nm_*/c^2$ ([[def-gr-schwarzschild-exterior-model]]). Then:\n\n**(a) Global metric and local components.** $M$ is a smooth four-manifold\nwithout boundary and $g$ is the smooth symmetric covariant $2$-tensor field\nglobally defined by\n\n$$g=-f\\,d(x^0)\\otimes d(x^0)+f^{-1}\\,dr\\otimes dr+r^2\\,\\iota^*\\gamma_{S^2}.$$\n\nIn every spherical-polar product chart $(x^0,r,\\vartheta,\\varphi)$ its\ncomponents are\n\n$$g_{00}=-f(r),\\qquad g_{11}=\\frac{1}{f(r)},\\qquad g_{22}=r^2,\\qquad g_{33}=r^2\\sin^2\\vartheta,\\qquad g_{ab}=0\\ (a\\ne b),$$\n\nwhere $f(r)=1-s/r$, $\\iota:M\\to S^2$ is the third projection and\n$\\gamma_{S^2}$ is the round metric of the unit sphere. The component display\nis local to the spherical-polar charts; the global tensor formula includes\nthe poles.\n\n**(b) Lorentzian signature.** At every $p\\in M$ the form $g_p$ is\nnondegenerate with inertia $(3,1,0)$; consequently $(M,g,N)$ is a smooth\nLorentzian four-manifold of signature $(-,+,+,+)$.\n\n**(c) Time orientation.** The global product field $N=\\partial_0$ is smooth\nand nowhere vanishing, and $g_p(N_p,N_p)=-f(r(p))<0$ at every $p$; it is\ntherefore a time orientation of $(M,g)$. The globally defined base fields\n$\\partial_0,\\partial_r$ are orthogonal with squared norms $-f$ and $f^{-1}$.\nIn a spherical-polar chart the angular coordinate fields are orthogonal with\npositive squared norms $r^2$ and $r^2\\sin^2\\vartheta$; at a pole use any\nangular chart and the positive-definite round metric instead.\n\n**(d) Domain of the coordinates.** Product charts form an atlas covering\n$M$. Spherical-polar charts are defined on the regular angular locus\n$0<\\vartheta<\\pi$ and omit the poles; pole charts are ordinary smooth charts\nof $S^2$. The functions $f$ and $f^{-1}$ are smooth on $r>s$, and the\nspherical-polar component functions are smooth on their chart domains.\n\n**(e) Conventions.** The global tensor identity holds on all of $M$; the\ndisplayed component identities are in spherical-polar product charts. In pole\ncharts the sphere block is $r^2$ times the round-metric component matrix.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-gr-metric-selected-connection",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(M,g,N)$ be a time-oriented smooth Lorentzian four-manifold\n([[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]]) and let $\\nabla$ be an affine connection\non $M$ ([[def-affine-connection-on-a-smooth-manifold]]). Smooth vector fields\nare smooth sections of the tangent bundle\n([[def-smooth-vector-field-as-a-tangent-bundle-section]]); as there, their use\nrests on the canonical smooth structure of $TM$, which assumes the axiom\n$\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ of [[def-countable-choice]]. This is the only use of that\naxiom in this definition.\n\n**(a) Metric compatibility.** The connection $\\nabla$ is **$g$-compatible**, or\n**compatible with the metric** $g$, when for all smooth vector fields $X,Y,Z$ on\n$M$ the identity of smooth functions\n\n$$X\\bigl(g(Y,Z)\\bigr)=g(\\nabla_XY,Z)+g(Y,\\nabla_XZ)$$\n\nholds on $M$. Here $g(Y,Z)$ is the smooth function $p\\mapsto g_p(Y_p,Z_p)$ and\n$X$ acts on functions as a derivation; the identity uses only the action of\n$\\nabla$ on vector fields and requires no extension of $\\nabla$ to tensor\nfields. When such an extension is available, this identity is the statement\n$\\nabla_Xg=0$, and the compatibility of $\\nabla$ with the inverse metric\n$g^{-1}$ follows; neither statement is part of this definition.\n\n**(b) Torsion freedom.** The **torsion** of $\\nabla$ is the tangent-valued\n$2$-tensor $T(X,Y)=\\nabla_XY-\\nabla_YX-[X,Y]$ of\n[[def-torsion-tensor-of-an-affine-connection]], with bracket as in\n[[def-lie-bracket-of-smooth-vector-fields]]. The connection is **torsion-free**\nwhen $T(X,Y)=0$ for all smooth $X,Y$, that is, when\n$\\nabla_XY-\\nabla_YX=[X,Y]$ identically.\n\n**(c) Metric-selected connections.** A **metric-selected connection** for\n$(M,g)$ is an affine connection on $M$ that is both torsion-free and\n$g$-compatible. Equivalently, it is an element of the class\n\n$$\\operatorname{LC}(M,g):=\\{\\nabla:\\nabla\\text{ is an affine connection on }M,\\ \\nabla\\text{ is torsion-free and }g\\text{-compatible}\\}.$$\n\nThe name records that the pair of conditions selects the connection out of all\naffine connections; it is the pair of conditions imposed on the connection that\nconventional general relativity uses, and the connection so selected is called\nthe **Levi–Civita connection** of $g$.\n\n\n**(d) Intrinsic construction before physical use.** For smooth local vector\nfields define the Koszul expression\n\n$$K(X,Y,Z):=Xg(Y,Z)+Yg(Z,X)-Zg(X,Y)+g([X,Y],Z)+g([Z,X],Y)-g([Y,Z],X).$$\n\nDefine $\\nabla^g_XY$ by $2g(\\nabla^g_XY,Z)=K(X,Y,Z)$ for every smooth local\n$Z$. The proof below establishes existence, smoothness, the affine-connection\nrules, metric compatibility, torsion freedom and uniqueness; it uses symmetry\nand nondegeneracy, not positive definiteness. Thus $\\operatorname{LC}(M,g)$\nhas this unique element, called the **Levi–Civita connection** of $g$. The later\nnondegenerate-metric theorem retains its fuller tensor-derivative statement.\nIts Christoffel symbols in any chart are defined by\n$\\nabla^g_{\\partial_b}\\partial_c=\\Gamma^a{}_{bc}\\partial_a$\n([[def-christoffel-symbols-of-an-affine-connection]]) and obey\n\n$$\\Gamma^a{}_{bc}=\\tfrac12 g^{ad}(\\partial_bg_{dc}+\\partial_cg_{bd}-\\partial_dg_{bc}),\\qquad a,b,c,d=0,1,2,3.$$\n\n**(e) Basic curvature and contractions.** The **curvature** of this connection\nis the operator of [[def-curvature-of-an-affine-connection]], with the fixed\nsign convention\n\n$$R^g(X,Y)Z:=\\nabla^g_X\\nabla^g_YZ-\\nabla^g_Y\\nabla^g_XZ-\\nabla^g_{[X,Y]}Z.$$\n\nThe general affine-connection theorem\n[[thm-curvature-is-a-type-one-three-tensor]] proves that it depends pointwise\nonly on $X,Y,Z$ and is a smooth tensor. Define its chart components by\n$R^g(\\partial_c,\\partial_d)\\partial_b=R^a{}_{bcd}\\partial_a$.\nDefine the **Ricci tensor** and **scalar curvature** pointwise by\n\n$$\\operatorname{Ric}_p(v,w):=\\operatorname{tr}(z\\mapsto R^g_p(z,w)v),\\qquad S(p):=\\operatorname{tr}(\\sharp_p\\circ\\operatorname{Ric}^{\\flat}_p),\\qquad \\operatorname{Ric}^{\\flat}_p(v):=\\operatorname{Ric}_p(v,\\cdot).$$\n\nHere $\\sharp$ is the inverse metric isomorphism and traces are the\nbasis-independent trace of [[def-trace-of-an-endomorphism]]. Equivalently,\n$\\operatorname{Ric}_{bd}=\\sum_aR^a{}_{bad}$ and\n$S=\\sum_{b,d}g^{bd}\\operatorname{Ric}_{bd}$, all sums from $0$ through $3$.\nThese are well-defined smooth fields by finite contraction of the smooth\ncomponents of $R^g$ and $g^{-1}$; basis independence follows from the trace\nand the intrinsic maps just defined. No symmetry identity or field equation\nis assumed here. Lowering the curvature output gives\n$R_{abcd}=g_{ae}R^e{}_{bcd}$, so the definitions agree with the full later\ncurvature item, which proves the remaining symmetries and coordinate formulas.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-gr-nondegenerate-levi-civita-and-tensor-derivative",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $M$ be a smooth four-manifold without boundary and let $g$ be a smooth\nsymmetric covariant $2$-tensor field on $M$ such that $g_p$ is nondegenerate at\nevery $p\\in M$ ([[def-gr-smooth-covariant-two-tensor-field]]). Assume\n$\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ for the canonical tangent-bundle smooth structure\n([[def-countable-choice]], [[def-smooth-vector-field-as-a-tangent-bundle-section]]).\nFor this general metric define $\\flat_p(v):=g_p(v,\\cdot)$,\n$\\sharp_p:=\\flat_p^{-1}$, and\n$g_p^{-1}(\\alpha,\\beta):=g_p(\\sharp_p\\alpha,\\sharp_p\\beta)$.\nNondegeneracy makes $\\flat_p$ injective and hence invertible between the\nfour-dimensional tangent space and its dual. In a chart, $g^{ab}$ are the\nentries of the inverse matrix of $(g_{ab})$. These definitions agree with\n[[def-gr-inverse-metric-and-musical-isomorphisms]] in its Lorentzian scope.\nHere and below repeated upper/lower indices are summed over $0,1,2,3$.\nThis holds in particular\nwhen $(M,g,N)$ is a time-oriented smooth Lorentzian four-manifold and $g$ is\nits metric ([[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]]). For smooth local vector fields\n$X,Y,Z$ put\n\n$$K(X,Y,Z):=X\\bigl(g(Y,Z)\\bigr)+Y\\bigl(g(Z,X)\\bigr)-Z\\bigl(g(X,Y)\\bigr)+g([X,Y],Z)+g([Z,X],Y)-g([Y,Z],X),$$\n\nwith brackets as in [[def-lie-bracket-of-smooth-vector-fields]].\n\n**(a) Existence and formulas.** There is exactly one affine connection\n$\\nabla$ on $M$ ([[def-affine-connection-on-a-smooth-manifold]]) satisfying\n\n$$2g(\\nabla_XY,Z)=K(X,Y,Z)\\qquad\\text{for all smooth local }X,Y,Z .$$\n\nIt is torsion-free and $g$-compatible, so it is a metric-selected connection\nfor $(M,g)$, meaning torsion-free and satisfying\n$Xg(Y,Z)=g(\\nabla_XY,Z)+g(Y,\\nabla_XZ)$ for smooth local fields\n(the conditions of [[def-gr-metric-selected-connection]] extended here to\nnondegenerate $g$); in every smooth chart its\nChristoffel symbols are\n\n$$\\Gamma^a{}_{bc}=\\tfrac12\\,g^{ad}\\bigl(\\partial_bg_{dc}+\\partial_cg_{bd}-\\partial_dg_{bc}\\bigr),$$\n\nwith $g^{ad}$ the inverse-metric components defined above\n([[def-christoffel-symbols-of-an-affine-connection]]).\n\n**(b) Uniqueness.** If an affine connection $\\nabla'$ on $M$ is torsion-free\nand $g$-compatible, then $\\nabla'=\\nabla$. Consequently the class\n$\\operatorname{LC}(M,g)$ of metric-selected connections has exactly one\nelement; it is the **Levi–Civita connection** of $g$.\n\n**(c) Induced derivative on tensor fields.** Let $\\nabla^*$ be the dual\nconnection on $T^*M$ and let the product connection of\n[[def-product-connection-on-tensor-and-hom-bundles]] act on\n$T^r_sM:=(TM)^{\\otimes r}\\otimes(T^*M)^{\\otimes s}$, for integers $r,s\\ge0$,\nconstructed by [[lem-finite-tensor-products-of-smooth-vector-bundles]]. A\ntype $(r,s)$ tensor at $p$ is a multilinear map\n$(T_p^*M)^r\\times(T_pM)^s\\to\\mathbb R$\n([[def-type-r-s-tensor-on-a-finite-dimensional-vector-space]]); a smooth\ntensor field is a section with smooth coefficients in each local product\nframe. The empty product is the scalar line. Its chart components are\n$T^{a_1\\cdots a_r}{}_{b_1\\cdots b_s}=T(dx^{a_1},\\ldots,dx^{a_r},\\partial_{b_1},\\ldots,\\partial_{b_s})$,\nwhere $dx^a$ is dual to $\\partial_a$. This agrees with\n[[def-gr-tensor-fields-and-components]] in the Lorentzian case. Put\n$T^{a_1\\cdots a_r}{}_{b_1\\cdots b_s;c}:=(\\nabla_{\\partial_c}T)^{a_1\\cdots a_r}{}_{b_1\\cdots b_s}$.\nThen, in every smooth chart, for\nevery smooth tensor field $T$ of type $(r,s)$,\n\n$$T^{a_1\\cdots a_r}{}_{b_1\\cdots b_s;c}=\\partial_cT^{a_1\\cdots a_r}{}_{b_1\\cdots b_s}+\\sum_{i=1}^{r}\\Gamma^{a_i}{}_{dc}\\,T^{a_1\\cdots d\\cdots a_r}{}_{b_1\\cdots b_s}-\\sum_{j=1}^{s}\\Gamma^{d}{}_{b_jc}\\,T^{a_1\\cdots a_r}{}_{b_1\\cdots d\\cdots b_s},$$\n\nwhere the slot marked $d$ replaces the named index; this derivative commutes\nwith every contraction in the sense of [[def-contraction-of-a-mixed-tensor]],\nand it annihilates the metric and the inverse metric:\n\n$$g_{ab;c}=0,\\qquad g^{ab}{}_{;c}=0 .$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-gr-killing-and-on-shell-matter-identities",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(M,g,N)$ be an oriented time-oriented smooth Lorentzian four-manifold with\nmetric-selected connection $\\nabla$\n([[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]],\n[[def-gr-metric-selected-connection]],\n[[thm-gr-nondegenerate-levi-civita-and-tensor-derivative]],\n[[def-oriented-smooth-manifold-and-oriented-chart]]).\n\n**(a) Characterization of Killing fields.** For every smooth vector field\n$\\xi$ ([[def-smooth-vector-field-as-a-tangent-bundle-section]]), with\n$\\xi_b=g_{bc}\\xi^c$ the lowered field,\n\n$$\\mathcal L_\\xi g=0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\\nabla_a\\xi_b+\\nabla_b\\xi_a=0 .$$\n\n**(b) Contraction along geodesics.** If $\\xi$ is a Killing field and\n$\\gamma:I\\to M$ is a geodesic with $D_t\\gamma'=0$ in its affine parameter\n([[def-geodesic-of-an-affine-connection]],\n[[def-covariant-derivative-along-a-curve]]), then $t\\mapsto\ng_{\\gamma(t)}(\\xi_{\\gamma(t)},\\gamma'(t))$ is constant on $I$.\n\n**(c) Conserved current.** Let $U\\subseteq M$ be open, let $T$ be a smooth\nsymmetric covariant $2$-tensor field on $U$\n([[def-gr-smooth-covariant-two-tensor-field]]) with\n$\\nabla^aT_{ab}=0$ on $U$, and let $\\xi$ be a Killing field on $U$. Then\n$J^{a}:=T^{ab}\\xi_b$ is a smooth vector field on $U$ with\n$\\nabla_aJ^a=0$; consequently the flux balance of\n[[thm-gr-density-divergence-and-finite-chain-flux]] clause (b) applies to $J$\non every smooth singular $4$-chain contained in $U$.\n\n**(d) On-shell conservation of the Hilbert tensor.** Let $c>0$, let\n$S:\\mathcal D\\to\\mathbb R$ be a model action with Hilbert stress-energy tensor\n$T$ at $(g,\\varphi)$ with respect to $c$\n([[def-gr-hilbert-stress-energy-tensor]]). For the flow diffeomorphisms $F$\nbelow, suppose the configuration set $\\mathcal X$ carries specified maps\n$P_F:\\mathcal X\\to\\mathcal X$ with $P_{\\mathrm{id}}=\\mathrm{id}$; write\n$F^*\\varphi:=P_F(\\varphi)$. For geometric matter fields these are the\nmodel's pullbacks; no pullback operation on an arbitrary set is implicit.\nFor **every** smooth compactly supported vector field $\\xi$, its flow\n$\\Phi_v$ is global\n([[def-complete-vector-field]], [[def-local-and-global-flow]]); this holds for\nevery compactly supported smooth field, using the axiom\n$\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ of [[def-countable-choice]] through\n[[thm-compactly-supported-vector-fields-are-complete]]. Write\n$G(u,v):=S(\\Phi_v^*g,\\Phi_u^*\\varphi)$ for the simultaneous pullback by the\nflow (this presupposes that the pairs lie in $\\mathcal D$, which is part of the\nhypotheses below). Suppose (H1)–(H4) hold for **every** such $\\xi$ (with\nits own $G$ and parameter neighborhood):\n\n- **(H1)** the map $G$ is defined on a neighborhood of $(0,0)$ in\n  $\\mathbb R^2$ and is differentiable at $(0,0)$;\n- **(H2)** $\\partial_uG(0,0)=0$ (the matter configuration $\\varphi$ satisfies\n  its model equations in this direction);\n- **(H3)** $\\partial_vG(0,0)=-\\frac{1}{2c}\\int_MT_{ab}q^{ab}\\mathrm{vol}_g$,\n  where $q^{ab}:=\\partial_v(\\Phi_v^*g)^{ab}|_{v=0}$ (the metric variation is\n  represented by the Hilbert tensor);\n- **(H4)** $G(s,s)=S(g,\\varphi)$ whenever $(s,s)$ lies in that neighborhood\n  (invariance of the action under simultaneous pullback by the flow).\n\nThen $\\nabla^aT_{ab}=0$ on $M$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-gr-killing-field",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(M,g,N)$ be a time-oriented smooth Lorentzian four-manifold\n([[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]]) and let $\\xi$ be a smooth vector field\non $M$ ([[def-smooth-vector-field-as-a-tangent-bundle-section]]). The **Lie\nderivative of the metric along $\\xi$**, written $\\mathcal L_\\xi g$, is the\nsmooth symmetric covariant $2$-tensor field obtained by the flow definition of\n[[def-lie-derivative-of-a-tensor-field]] applied to $g$: for the local flow\n$\\Phi_t$ of $\\xi$,\n\n$$\\mathcal L_\\xi g:=\\frac{d}{dt}\\Big|_{t=0}\\Phi_t^*g .$$\n\nThe field $\\xi$ is a **Killing field** of $(M,g)$ (an **isometry generator**,\nor a **Killing vector**) when\n\n$$\\mathcal L_\\xi g=0 .$$\n\nThe equivalent local characterization\n\n$$\\mathcal L_\\xi g=0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\\nabla_a\\xi_b+\\nabla_b\\xi_a=0,$$\n\nwhere $\\nabla$ is the metric-selected connection of $g$\n([[def-gr-metric-selected-connection]],\n[[thm-gr-nondegenerate-levi-civita-and-tensor-derivative]]) and\n$\\xi_b:=g_{bc}\\xi^c$ is the lowered field, is proved in\n[[thm-gr-killing-and-on-shell-matter-identities]] clause (a); the definition\nitself asserts neither direction. Whether a given metric has Killing fields is\na property of that metric: neither existence nor uniqueness of a Killing field\nis asserted here, and a general metric has none beyond the trivial field\n$\\xi=0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-lie-derivative-of-a-tensor-field",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "If $X$ has local flow $\\Phi_t$ and $T$ is a smooth tensor field of type $(r,s)$, its Lie derivative is $$\\mathcal L_XT=\\left.\\frac d{dt}\\right|_{t=0}\\Phi_t^*T,$$ on every local flow domain where this derivative is defined. Here the pullback by the local diffeomorphism $\\Phi_t$ acts by $d\\Phi_{-t}$ on each contravariant slot and by $d\\Phi_t$ on each covariant slot.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-geodesic-of-an-affine-connection",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $M$ be a smooth manifold without boundary with affine connection $\\nabla$, and let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an interval with nonempty interior. A smooth curve $\\gamma:I\\to M$ is an **affinely parametrized geodesic** when\n$$D_t\\gamma'(t)=0\\qquad(t\\in I),$$\nwith one-sided interpretation at an included endpoint. Constant curves are geodesics. Unless another parametrization is explicitly stated, “geodesic” means affinely parametrized geodesic.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "In each equatorial spherical chart the components in [F1] are independent of $x^0$ and $\\varphi$, and the coordinate components of $\\partial_0$ and the axial generator $\\partial_\\varphi$ are constant. The flow pullback derivative in [F2] therefore gives $\\mathcal L_{\\partial_0}g=\\mathcal L_{\\partial_\\varphi}g=0$, so both fields are locally Killing. By the conservation part of [F2] and the affine geodesic equation [F3], $-g(\\partial_0,\\gamma')=f\\,dx^0/d\\lambda=E$ and $g(\\partial_\\varphi,\\gamma')=r^2d\\varphi/d\\lambda=L$ are constant on each chart interval; longitude charts differ by additive constants, so these values agree on overlaps and are constant along $\\gamma$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "At $\\vartheta=\\pi/2$, the null condition and [F1] give $0=-f\\left(\\frac{dx^0}{d\\lambda}\\right)^2+f^{-1}\\left(\\frac{dr}{d\\lambda}\\right)^2+r^2\\left(\\frac{d\\varphi}{d\\lambda}\\right)^2.$ Substitute $dx^0/d\\lambda=E/f$ and $d\\varphi/d\\lambda=L/r^2$, then multiply by $f>0$. The result is $(dr/d\\lambda)^2=E^2[1-(L/E)^2f/r^2]$, the asserted radial first integral.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Differentiating $H(r)=r^{-2}-sr^{-3}$ yields $H'(r)=-2r^{-3}+3sr^{-4}=(3s-2r)/r^4$. Since $r^4>0$, this derivative is positive on $(s,3s/2)$ and negative on $(3s/2,\\infty)$; hence the unique maximum is at $3s/2$. Substitution gives $H(3s/2)=4/(27s^2)$, and solving $b^2H(3s/2)=1$ gives $b_{\\rm c}=3\\sqrt3s/2$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "The right side of the radial first integral vanishes at a radius precisely when $b^2H(r)=1$. Since $H$ is positive on $r>s$, tends to zero at both ends, and has the maximum computed in the preceding step, the threshold for the existence of an exterior root is exactly $b_{\\rm c}$. This is a first-integral statement only; it asserts no global scattering continuation.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The statement assumes s>0 and r>s; step 3.1 constructs the maximum at the nonempty radius 3s/2."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The statement permits L=0 and b=0; step 3.1 classifies the radial first-integral zero condition."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
