    "pthm-rpm-schwarzschild-null-scattering": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "pthm-gr-schwarzschild-vacuum-model",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Adopt the postulates [[post-gr-spacetime-and-classical-domain]],\n[[post-gr-field-and-matter-assumptions]],\n[[post-gr-einstein-field-law]] and\n[[post-gr-ideal-clocks-and-free-fall]] of the classical gravitational\nframework, with the field law in its direct-equation presentation and the unit\nconventions of [[def-gr-si-unit-and-coordinate-conventions]]. Let\n$(M,g,N)$ be the Schwarzschild exterior datum of mass parameter $m_*>0$ with\nSchwarzschild radius $s=2G_Nm_*/c^2$ and static congruence\n$n=f^{-1/2}\\partial_0$, $f(r)=1-s/r$\n([[def-gr-schwarzschild-exterior-model]]), and let the model's stress-energy\ntensor be the vacuum prescription\n\n$$T:=0\\qquad\\text{on }M,\\qquad \\Lambda:=0 .$$\n\nThen all statements below are conditional deductions inside the model so\nspecified; they assert nothing about nature.\n\n**(a) The Schwarzschild vacuum model.** $(M,g,N)$ satisfies the spacetime\npostulate, $T=0$ is a smooth symmetric stress-energy tensor field on the\nmatter domain $M$ in the sense of [[def-gr-stress-energy-tensor-field]], the\nprescription is admissible, and the adopted field law\n$G_{ab}+\\Lambda g_{ab}=\\kappa T_{ab}$ holds identically on $M$. A **vacuum solution with zero cosmological parameter** here means\nprecisely a datum satisfying that equation with $T=0$ and $\\Lambda=0$;\nthis datum is such a solution, read as a model of the framework. The model is not locally flat: its curvature tensor is nowhere\nzero, while its Ricci tensor and Einstein tensor vanish identically\n([[lem-gr-schwarzschild-ricci-and-einstein-tensors]] clause (d)).\n\n**(b) Static clock readings.** The integral curves of $n$ are the static\nobserver worldlines at constant radius $r_0$\n([[lem-gr-schwarzschild-static-congruence-and-clock-rates]] clause (b));\nadopting the clock rule of [[post-gr-ideal-clocks-and-free-fall]] clause (i),\nthe reading difference of an ideal clock carried along such a worldline\nbetween two events whose coordinate times differ by $\\Delta t$ is\n\n$$\\Delta\\tau=\\sqrt{f(r_0)}\\,\\Delta t=\\sqrt{1-\\frac{s}{r_0}}\\;\\Delta t,$$\n\nso two static clocks at radii $r_1,r_2$ compared over the same coordinate\ninterval with $\\Delta t\\ne0$ satisfy $\\Delta\\tau_1/\\Delta\\tau_2=\\sqrt{f(r_1)/f(r_2)}$. The static\nworldlines are not geodesics: their acceleration is\n$a=\\frac{s}{2r^2}\\partial_r\\ne0$, so the model assigns them to accelerated\n(constrained) bodies and not to freely falling ones.\n\n**(c) The mass parameter.** The parameter $m_*$ enters the model only through\n$s=2G_Nm_*/c^2$ and through the quantities below; the framework reads $m_*$ as\nthe mass parameter of the model in kilograms\n([[def-gr-si-unit-and-coordinate-conventions]] clause (a),\n[[def-gr-schwarzschild-exterior-model]] clause (a)):\n\n1. Let $\\gamma:I\\to M$ be a smooth future timelike geodesic, parametrized\n   by proper time $\\tau$ on an interval with nonempty interior, with\n   $r\\circ\\gamma\\equiv r_0>\\frac{3s}{2}$. Put $\\sigma:=\\iota\\circ\\gamma:I\\to S^2$\n   and $t:=(x^0\\circ\\gamma)/c$. Its **angular speed with respect to $t$** is\n   $\\Omega:=\\sqrt{\\gamma_{S^2}(d\\sigma/dt,d\\sigma/dt)}$, where\n   $d\\sigma/dt:=\\sigma'(\\tau)/t'(\\tau)$ and $t'(\\tau)>0$ by future\n   timelikeness. It has units $\\mathrm s^{-1}$ and satisfies\n   $\\Omega^2=G_Nm_*/r_0^3$ in every angular orientation. For an equatorial\n   curve, $\\vartheta=\\pi/2$ and $\\Omega^2=(d\\varphi/dt)^2$ in local polar\n   charts ([[lem-gr-schwarzschild-circular-orbits-and-photon-sphere]]\n   clause (a) and proof step 2.2). This is the Kepler-like law in which\n   $m_*$ plays the role of the central mass;\n2. in the isotropic chart of\n   [[lem-gr-schwarzschild-isotropic-coordinate-form]], with\n   $a=s/(4\\rho)$, the exact identity\n   $g_{00}+1=1-\\bigl((1-a)/(1+a)\\bigr)^2=4a/(1+a)^2$ gives\n   $$g_{00}+1=+\\frac{s}{\\rho}+O(\\rho^{-2})=+\\frac{2G_Nm_*}{c^2\\rho}+O(\\rho^{-2}).$$\n   Under the weak-field convention $g_{00}=-(1+2\\Phi/c^2)$ to leading order\n   ([[def-gr-stationary-weak-field-data]]), this is the coefficient\n   $-2\\Phi_N/c^2$ for the explicit exterior Newtonian potential\n   $\\Phi_N(\\rho):=-G_Nm_*/\\rho$ in the comparison reading of\n   [[def-gr-newtonian-comparison-model-and-identification]].\n\nThe reading is a statement about the model's own identities and the adopted\nunit and weak-field conventions; no numerical value of $G_N$ or $m_*$, no\nNewtonian source model realized by matter and no comparison with any reported\nmass is asserted.\n\n**(d) Scope.** The model is an idealized vacuum model of the framework, not a\nclaim about the actual universe: no observation, apparatus, calibration or\nuncertainty is used, and no comparison with data is made. The datum solves the\nfield law with the chosen parameters $(m_*,\\Lambda,T)=(m_*,0,0)$; other\nsolutions of the same field law exist and are not excluded, and the exterior\ndomain $r>s$ contains no horizon or interior region, whose treatment is the\nsubject of the extension and Kruskal items of this page. The physical\ninterpretation of coordinates, clocks and bodies remains the adopted one of\nthe cited postulates.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "post-gr-ideal-clocks-and-free-fall",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Adopt as physical assumptions of the classical gravitational framework, in\naddition to [[post-gr-spacetime-and-classical-domain]] and the matter-content\nassumptions of the framework, the following rules by which ideal clocks, test\nbodies and light couple to the metric $g$ of\n[[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]].\n\n**(i) Ideal clocks.** An **ideal clock** is an idealized physical device\ncarried along a future timelike worldline ([[def-gr-timelike-worldline-and-proper-time]])\nwhose readings are assumed to reproduce the proper time of that worldline:\nthe difference of two readings taken at two events of the worldline is the\nproper-time increment between those events as defined by the metric, and it is\nindependent of the construction of the clock. Two ideal clocks moving along\nthe same worldline differ only by an additive constant (their choice of zero);\ndifferent worldlines generally accrue different proper times between the same\npair of events. The physical constant $c$ and the unit of duration are those\nof [[def-gr-si-unit-and-coordinate-conventions]].\n\n**(ii) Free fall of massive test bodies.** A **test body** is an uncharged\nidealized body whose internal structure is small compared with the scale over\nwhich the geometry of the model varies and whose gravitational self-field is\nnegligible within the model. An **ideal freely falling massive test body** is\nassumed to have a worldline that is a future timelike geodesic of the\nmetric-selected connection ([[def-gr-metric-selected-connection]],\n[[thm-gr-nondegenerate-levi-civita-and-tensor-derivative]],\n[[def-geodesic-of-an-affine-connection]]), traversed with physical\nfour-velocity $U=d\\gamma/d\\tau$ satisfying\n$g_{\\gamma(\\tau)}(U,U)=-c^2$; proper time is an affine parameter of the\nworldline ([[lem-gr-proper-time-reparametrization-and-four-velocity]]). The\nworldline is determined by the event at which the body is released together\nwith its four-velocity there, by the uniqueness of the geodesic initial-value\nproblem ([[thm-gr-local-geodesics-and-exponential-map]]); no force, charge, or\nrest mass of the body enters this rule.\n\n**(iii) Light rays.** In the geometric-optics approximation an **ideal light\nray** propagating in the model is assumed to be a future null geodesic of the\nmetric-selected connection\n([[def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors]],\n[[def-geodesic-of-an-affine-connection]]). A null worldline carries no proper\ntime and no four-velocity in the sense of\n[[def-gr-timelike-worldline-and-proper-time]]; within the approximation the\npropagation of an ideal ray is independent of its frequency, and only the\nray's direction in the tangent space is fixed, not a scale along it.\n\n**(iv) Scope and exclusions.** The three rules are adopted in the test-field\nregime of the smooth classical model: the clocks, bodies and rays do not\nback-react on the geometry, and the geometry varies smoothly on the scales\nrelevant to them. Configurations requiring the body's own gravitational field,\nradiation reaction, an electromagnetic or other self-force, finite-size or\ntidal couplings beyond the test-body idealization, non-geometric-optics\npropagation, dispersion or dissipation of light, or quantum effects are\noutside these assumptions and require additional model content. The\nassumptions do not assert that any real clock, body or light ray is exactly\nideal, nor do they attach measured readings to any model.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-rpm-schwarzschild-null-geodesic-reduction",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(M,g,N)$ be the Schwarzschild exterior datum with $s>0$ and $f(r)=1-s/r$ on $r>s$. Let $\\gamma(\\lambda)$ be an affinely parametrized null geodesic of its metric-selected connection whose image lies in the equatorial plane $\\vartheta=\\pi/2$, and choose the orientation so that\n\n$$E:=f(r)\\frac{dx^0}{d\\lambda}>0,\\qquad L:=r^2\\frac{d\\varphi}{d\\lambda}\\ge0.$$\n\nBoth quantities are constant along $\\gamma$. With impact parameter $b:=L/E\\ge0$ and $H(r):=f(r)/r^2$, its radial equation is\n\n$$\\left(\\frac{dr}{d\\lambda}\\right)^2=E^2\\bigl(1-b^2H(r)\\bigr).$$\n\nMoreover, $H'(r)=(3s-2r)/r^4$, so $H$ has its unique exterior maximum at $r_{\\rm ph}=3s/2$, with $H(r_{\\rm ph})=4/(27s^2)$. Thus the critical impact parameter for a radial turning point is $b_{\\rm c}=3\\sqrt3\\,s/2$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "2.3",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-gr-local-geodesics-and-exponential-map",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(M,g,N)$ be a time-oriented smooth Lorentzian four-manifold and let\n$\\nabla$ be its metric-selected connection, whose existence and uniqueness are\nsupplied by [[thm-gr-nondegenerate-levi-civita-and-tensor-derivative]]; its\nChristoffel symbols are the smooth chart functions of\n[[def-christoffel-symbols-of-an-affine-connection]]. Recall that a **geodesic**\nis a curve with $D_t\\gamma'=0$ in its parameter\n([[def-geodesic-of-an-affine-connection]],\n[[def-covariant-derivative-along-a-curve]]).\n\n**(a) Local existence, uniqueness and maximality.** For every $(p,v)\\in TM$\nthere is a unique maximal geodesic $\\gamma_{p,v}:I_{p,v}\\to M$ with\n$\\gamma_{p,v}(0)=p$ and $\\gamma_{p,v}'(0)=v$; its domain $I_{p,v}$ is an open\ninterval containing $0$ and is not asserted to be all of $\\mathbb R$.\n\n**(b) Smooth dependence.** The set\n$\\mathcal G:=\\{(t,p,v):t\\in I_{p,v}\\}\\subseteq\\mathbb R\\times TM$ is open, and\nthe evaluation map $(t,p,v)\\mapsto\\gamma_{p,v}(t)$ is smooth on $\\mathcal G$.\n\n**(c) Scaling.** For every $s\\in\\mathbb R$ and every $t\\in\\mathbb R$,\n$t\\in I_{p,sv}$ if and only if $st\\in I_{p,v}$, and then\n$\\gamma_{p,sv}(t)=\\gamma_{p,v}(st)$.\n\n**(d) The exponential map.** Define $\\mathcal E_p:=\\{v\\in T_pM:1\\in I_{p,v}\\}$\nand $\\exp_p:\\mathcal E_p\\to M$, $\\exp_p(v):=\\gamma_{p,v}(1)$, and let\n$\\psi_p:T_pM\\to\\mathbb R^4$ be the coordinate isomorphism of a basis\n$e_0,\\dots,e_3$ of $T_pM$. Then $\\mathcal E_p$ is open and star-shaped about\n$0$ (if $v\\in\\mathcal E_p$ and $0\\le s\\le1$, then $sv\\in\\mathcal E_p$),\n$\\exp_p$ is smooth, $\\exp_p(0)=p$, and, under the canonical identification\n$T_0(T_pM)\\cong T_pM$ induced by the chart $\\psi_p$ on $T_pM$,\n$$d(\\exp_p)_0=\\operatorname{id}_{T_pM}.$$\nConsequently there is a star-shaped open neighborhood $W_p\\subseteq\\mathcal E_p$\nof $0$ such that $\\exp_p|_{W_p}$ is a diffeomorphism onto an open subset of\n$M$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-cm-compact-continuation-for-locally-lipschitz-odes",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n\\ge1$, let $D\\subseteq\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^n$ be open, and let $F:D\\to\\mathbb R^n$ be continuous and locally Lipschitz in the state variable, locally uniformly in time, in the sense of [[def-locally-lipschitz-in-the-state-variable]]. Let $t_0<b$ be reals, let $x_0\\in\\mathbb R^n$ with $(t_0,x_0)\\in D$, and let $x:[t_0,b)\\to\\mathbb R^n$ be a solution of the initial value problem\n\n$$x'(t)=F\\bigl(t,x(t)\\bigr),\\qquad x(t_0)=x_0$$\n\non $[t_0,b)$ ([[def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution]]). Suppose there is a compact set $K\\subseteq D$ and a real $\\delta>0$ such that\n\n$$\\bigl(t,x(t)\\bigr)\\in K\\qquad\\text{for every }t\\in(b-\\delta,b)\\cap[t_0,b).$$\n\nThen:\n\n1. there is $\\varepsilon>0$ and a solution $\\tilde x:[t_0,b+\\varepsilon)\\to\\mathbb R^n$ of the same initial value problem with $\\tilde x(t)=x(t)$ for all $t\\in[t_0,b)$; in particular $\\tilde x(b)=\\lim_{t\\uparrow b}x(t)$ exists and lies in the state projection of $K$;\n2. if moreover $D=\\mathbb R\\times U$ for an open $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ and $F$ is autonomous, $F(t,z)=G(z)$ with $G:U\\to\\mathbb R^n$ continuous and locally Lipschitz in the state variable ([[def-locally-lipschitz-in-the-state-variable]]), and if there is a compact $K_0\\subseteq U$ with $x(t)\\in K_0$ for every $t\\in(b-\\delta,b)\\cap[t_0,b)$, then the extension conclusion of clause 1 holds as well.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-gr-kruskal-model-data",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "This item assumes the axiom of countable choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ through\nthe tangent-bundle convention of [[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]]\nclause (c); the product-smooth-structure supplier has no choice hypothesis\n([[def-countable-choice]]); no further choice principle is used.\n\n**(a) Parameters.** Let $c>0$, $G_N>0$ and $m_*>0$ be the constants and mass\nparameter of the Schwarzschild exterior datum, and put\n$s:=2G_Nm_*/c^2>0$ ([[def-gr-schwarzschild-exterior-model]] clause (a)). The\n**Kruskal model datum of mass parameter $m_*$** is constructed from these\ndata as follows.\n\n**(b) Domain and smooth structure.** Let\n$\\Omega:=\\{(U,V)\\in\\mathbb R^2:UV<1\\}$ and let\n$\\mathcal K:=\\Omega\\times S^2$, where $S^2\\subset\\mathbb R^3$ is the unit\nEuclidean sphere, with the product smooth structure\n([[prop-products-of-smooth-manifolds-have-a-canonical-product-smooth-structure]],\n[[def-euclidean-spheres-and-closed-balls]]). It is a smooth four-manifold\nwithout boundary. Write $U,V:\\mathcal K\\to\\mathbb R$ for the first two\nprojections and $\\iota:\\mathcal K\\to S^2$ for the third. A **Kruskal product\nchart** uses any smooth chart on $S^2$. In a spherical-polar Kruskal chart the\nangular coordinates satisfy $0<\\vartheta<\\pi$ and\n$\\gamma_{S^2}=\\operatorname{diag}(1,\\sin^2\\vartheta)_{ij}$; these charts omit\nthe two poles, which are covered by ordinary sphere charts.\n\n**(c) The radial function.** The datum carries a function\n$r:\\mathcal K\\to(0,\\infty)$, the **Kruskal radial function**, characterized by\nthe implicit relation\n\n$$U\\,V=-\\Bigl(\\frac{r}{s}-1\\Bigr)e^{r/s}\\qquad\\text{at every point of }\\mathcal K .$$\n\nThat for every $(U,V)\\in\\Omega$ there is exactly one $r(U,V)>0$ satisfying\nthis relation, and that $r$ is smooth, is not asserted here; it is proved in\n[[lem-gr-kruskal-model-properties]] on this page, the justification record of\nthis definition. The value $r=0$ is not attained on $\\mathcal K$, and\n$r=s$ exactly on the axes $U=0$ or $V=0$.\n\n**(d) The metric.** Define the metric globally by\n\n$$g_{\\mathcal K}=-\\frac{2s^3}{r}e^{-r/s}(dU\\otimes dV+dV\\otimes dU)+r^2\\,\\iota^*\\gamma_{S^2}.$$\n\nIn every spherical-polar Kruskal chart its component functions are\n\n$$g_{UV}:=g_{VU}:=-\\frac{2s^3}{r}e^{-r/s},\\qquad g_{\\vartheta\\vartheta}:=r^2,\\qquad g_{\\varphi\\varphi}:=r^2\\sin^2\\vartheta,\\qquad g_{UU}:=g_{VV}:=g_{U\\vartheta}:=g_{U\\varphi}:=g_{V\\vartheta}:=g_{V\\varphi}:=0,$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the function of clause (c) evaluated at the point. This global\nexpression defines a smooth symmetric covariant $2$-tensor field on\n$\\mathcal K$ once the smoothness of $r$ is established by\n[[lem-gr-kruskal-model-properties]]. In another sphere chart the angular block\nis $r^2(\\gamma_{ij})$. That $g_{\\mathcal K}$ is Lorentzian is proved, not\nasserted, in the same lemma.\n\n**(e) Time orientation.** Define the field\n$N_{\\mathcal K}:=\\partial_U+\\partial_V$ on $\\mathcal K$. Its component\nexpression is the same in every Kruskal chart; that it is a smooth timelike\nfield and hence a time orientation of $g_{\\mathcal K}$ is proved in\n[[lem-gr-kruskal-model-properties]].\n\n**(f) Scope.** The datum is a geometric object: the definition asserts\nnothing about whether $g_{\\mathcal K}$ satisfies any field equation, about\nits causal structure, about the maximality of the extension it provides, or\nabout nature. The comparison with the exterior datum, the vacuum property and\nthe causal structure are the content of the two following items on this page.\nThe region $\\{UV=1\\}$, where the implicit relation of clause (c) has no\nsolution with $r>0$, is not part of $\\mathcal K$ and is not appended to it by\nthis definition.",
          "uses": [
            "2.3",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-gr-kruskal-causal-escape-and-trapping",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(\\mathcal K,r,g_{\\mathcal K},N_{\\mathcal K})$ be the Kruskal model datum of\nmass parameter $m_*>0$ with Schwarzschild radius $s=2G_Nm_*/c^2>0$\n([[def-gr-kruskal-model-data]], [[lem-gr-kruskal-model-properties]]), let\n$(U,V,\\xi^2,\\xi^3)$ be any Kruskal product chart, and let $\\gamma_{ij}$ be\nthe round-sphere components in its angular chart. In a spherical-polar\nKruskal chart, $(\\xi^2,\\xi^3)=(\\vartheta,\\varphi)$ and\n$\\gamma_{ij}=\\operatorname{diag}(1,\\sin^2\\vartheta)$. Define\n$A:\\mathcal K\\to(0,\\infty)$ by\n\n$$A:=\\frac{4s^3e^{-r/s}}{r},$$\n\nso that in a product chart $g_{UV}=g_{VU}=-A/2$, $g_{ij}=r^2\\gamma_{ij}$ and\nthe mixed base-angular components vanish. In a spherical-polar chart this\nreads $g_{\\vartheta\\vartheta}=r^2$,\n$g_{\\varphi\\varphi}=r^2\\sin^2\\vartheta$. For a\n$C^1$ curve $\\eta:J\\to\\mathcal K$ with $J$ a nonempty interval write\n$\\dot\\eta=(\\dot U,\\dot V,\\dot\\xi^2,\\dot\\xi^3)$ for its coordinate\ncomponents, and call $\\eta$ **future-directed causal** when\n$g_{\\mathcal K}(\\dot\\eta,\\dot\\eta)\\le0$ and\n$g_{\\mathcal K}(\\dot\\eta,N_{\\mathcal K})<0$ at every parameter value. Then:\n\n**(a) Causal cone criterion.** At every $p\\in\\mathcal K$ and for every\n$X\\in T_p\\mathcal K$ with chart components $(X^U,X^V,X^\\omega)$ one has:\n$X$ is causal and future-directed if and only if\n\n$$X^U\\ge0,\\qquad X^V\\ge0,\\qquad X^U+X^V>0,\\qquad A(p)\\,X^UX^V\\ \\ge\\ r(p)^2\\,\\gamma_p(X^\\omega,X^\\omega),$$\n\nwhere $\\gamma_p(X^\\omega,X^\\omega)$ is the round-sphere squared norm of the\nangular part. In particular every future-directed causal tangent has both\n$X^U\\ge0$ and $X^V\\ge0$.\n\n**(b) Monotonicity.** Along every future-directed causal $C^1$ curve the\nfunctions $U$ and $V$ are nondecreasing, and along every past-directed causal\n$C^1$ curve (where $g(\\dot\\eta,\\dot\\eta)\\le0$ and\n$g(\\dot\\eta,N_{\\mathcal K})>0$) they are nonincreasing.\n\n**(c) Radial null geodesics and their affine parameters.** For every\n$(U_0,V_0,\\omega_0)\\in\\mathcal K$ there is a unique maximal solution\n$\\lambda\\mapsto(U(\\lambda),V_0,\\omega_0)$ of\n$\\dfrac{dU}{d\\lambda}=\\dfrac{1}{A(U(\\lambda),V_0)}$, $U(0)=U_0$; it is an\naffinely parametrized null geodesic of $g_{\\mathcal K}$ with\n$g(\\dot\\eta,\\dot\\eta)=0$. The same holds for\n$\\lambda\\mapsto(U_0,V(\\lambda),\\omega_0)$ with\n$\\dfrac{dV}{d\\lambda}=\\dfrac{1}{A(U_0,V(\\lambda))}$. Both are\nfuture-directed, and $\\lambda$ increases strictly with $U$, respectively with\n$V$, because $A>0$. Moreover $\\partial_U r=-\\dfrac{s^2Ve^{-r/s}}{r}$ and\n$\\partial_Vr=-\\dfrac{s^2Ue^{-r/s}}{r}$ at every point of $\\mathcal K$.\n\n**(d) Escape horizon.** Call a future-directed causal $C^1$ curve $\\eta$\n**escaping to the right exterior** when there is a parameter value after which\n$\\eta$ stays in the open quadrant $\\{U<0,\\ V>0\\}$ and\n$r(\\eta(t))\\to+\\infty$ as $t\\to+\\infty$ (a point with $U\\ge0$ cannot reach the\nquadrant $\\{U<0,V>0\\}$ at all, by (b), and this is the sense in which the\nhorizon is a one-way membrane here). Then:\n\n1. no future-directed causal curve issued at a point $p$ with $U(p)\\ge0$\n   escapes to the right exterior;\n2. every point $p$ with $U(p)<0$ admits such a curve: for\n   $t>0$ the curve $\\eta_p(t):=(U(p),\\,V(p)+t,\\,\\omega(p))$ is a future-directed\n   null $C^1$ curve, it stays in\n   $\\{U<0,V>0\\}$ for $t>1+|V(p)|$ and satisfies\n   $r(\\eta_p(t))\\to+\\infty$ as $t\\to+\\infty$.\n\nConsequently the exact nonescaping set of the datum is $\\{U\\ge0\\}$ with\nboundary the null surface $\\{U=0\\}$, and the past-directed dual statement\nsingles out $\\{V=0\\}$ as the boundary of the region admitting past-directed\nescape to the right exterior: here escape means eventual residence in\n$\\{U<0,V>0\\}$ with $r\\to+\\infty$ as the past-directed parameter tends to\n$+\\infty$.\n\n**(e) Decreasing areal radius.** If $U(p)>0$ and $V(p)>0$, then along both\nfuture directions of the radial null geodesics of (c) through $p$ the function\n$r$ is strictly decreasing as their affine parameter $\\lambda$ increases:\non $\\lambda\\mapsto(U_0,V(\\lambda),\\omega_0)$ and on\n$\\lambda\\mapsto(U(\\lambda),V_0,\\omega_0)$.\n\n**(f) Scope.** All statements are geometric properties of the smooth Kruskal\ndatum on $\\mathcal K$; no maximality, geodesic completeness or uniqueness is\nclaimed, the set $\\{UV=1\\}$ (where $r=0$) is not part of $\\mathcal K$, and no\nstatement is made about the physical interpretation of any curve. The escape\nnotion of (d) is defined by the displayed conditions on $U,V$ and $r$ and does\nnot presuppose a conformal infinity.",
          "uses": [
            "2.3",
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Choose $R>r_{\\rm ph}$ so $b^2H(R)<1$. At $r=R$, set $\\theta=\\pi/2$, $\\dot x^0=E/f(R)$, $\\dot r=-E\\sqrt{1-b^2H(R)}$, $\\dot\\theta=0$, and $\\dot\\varphi=L/R^2$. The null condition in [F1] is satisfied, the tangent is future directed because $\\dot x^0>0$, and [F2] gives a local geodesic. On the past branch $\\dot r\\to-E$ as $r\\to\\infty$, so $\\int_R^\\infty dr/[E\\sqrt{1-b^2H(r)}]=\\infty$; compact continuation on finite radial intervals supplies an incoming ray from infinity for every $b\\ge0$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "By [F1], $H$ increases on $(s,r_{\\rm ph})$, decreases on $(r_{\\rm ph},\\infty)$, and vanishes at both exterior ends. If $b>b_{\\rm c}$, then $b^2H(r_{\\rm ph})>1$, so there is one root $r_-\\in(s,r_{\\rm ph})$ and one simple outer root $r_*>r_{\\rm ph}$. The affine time from any finite large radius to $r_*$ is finite because near the simple root $[E\\sqrt{1-b^2H(r)}]^{-1}=O((r-r_*)^{-1/2})$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "If $b=b_{\\rm c}$, direct factorization gives $1-b_{\\rm c}^2H(r)=(2r-3s)^2(r+3s)/(4r^3)$. The incoming radial speed vanishes linearly as $r\\downarrow r_{\\rm ph}$; hence the affine integral diverges like $\\int dr/(r-r_{\\rm ph})$. The angular integrand has the same simple-pole behavior, so the ray approaches the photon sphere only at infinite affine parameter and accumulates logarithmically divergent azimuth.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-3",
          "claim": "If $0\\le b<b_{\\rm c}$, then $b^2H(r)<1$ for every $r>s$ by [F1]. The inward branch has no turning point and reaches the exterior boundary $r=s$ in finite affine parameter because $1-b^2H(r)\\to1$ there. The exterior calculation ends at that boundary. If a future continuation is placed in the Kruskal model and enters $U\\ge0$, [F3] prevents it from returning to the right exterior.",
          "step": "2.3",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "For $b>b_{\\rm c}$, [F2] extends the incoming ray through the outer turn: the radial first integral gives $\\ddot r=-E^2b^2H'(r)/2>0$ at $r_*$, so it leaves with $\\dot r>0$. On every finite segment with $r\\ge r_*$, the conserved quantities bound $|\\dot r|$, $|\\dot x^0|=E/f(r)$ and $|\\dot\\varphi|=L/r^2$; the state stays in a compact subset of the smooth exterior tangent domain and cannot end at a finite affine parameter. On the outgoing branch $\\dot r\\to E>0$, while $|\\dot r|\\le E$, so it reaches arbitrarily large radii only at infinite affine parameter. Also $|d\\varphi/dr|=b/[r^2\\sqrt{1-b^2H(r)}]$ is $O((r-r_*)^{-1/2})$ at the turn and $O(r^{-2})$ at infinity; integrating both halves gives the convergent formula in clause 1.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "The simple outer root, critical double root and no-turn regimes in 2.1–2.3 give all three clauses. Only the last clause uses the separate Kruskal continuation, and no observation or apparatus model is used.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 2.2",
            "step 2.3",
            "step 3.1",
            "A1",
            "F3",
            "given"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
