    "pthm-rpm-static-clock-redshift": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "pthm-gr-schwarzschild-vacuum-model",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Adopt the postulates [[post-gr-spacetime-and-classical-domain]],\n[[post-gr-field-and-matter-assumptions]],\n[[post-gr-einstein-field-law]] and\n[[post-gr-ideal-clocks-and-free-fall]] of the classical gravitational\nframework, with the field law in its direct-equation presentation and the unit\nconventions of [[def-gr-si-unit-and-coordinate-conventions]]. Let\n$(M,g,N)$ be the Schwarzschild exterior datum of mass parameter $m_*>0$ with\nSchwarzschild radius $s=2G_Nm_*/c^2$ and static congruence\n$n=f^{-1/2}\\partial_0$, $f(r)=1-s/r$\n([[def-gr-schwarzschild-exterior-model]]), and let the model's stress-energy\ntensor be the vacuum prescription\n\n$$T:=0\\qquad\\text{on }M,\\qquad \\Lambda:=0 .$$\n\nThen all statements below are conditional deductions inside the model so\nspecified; they assert nothing about nature.\n\n**(a) The Schwarzschild vacuum model.** $(M,g,N)$ satisfies the spacetime\npostulate, $T=0$ is a smooth symmetric stress-energy tensor field on the\nmatter domain $M$ in the sense of [[def-gr-stress-energy-tensor-field]], the\nprescription is admissible, and the adopted field law\n$G_{ab}+\\Lambda g_{ab}=\\kappa T_{ab}$ holds identically on $M$. A **vacuum solution with zero cosmological parameter** here means\nprecisely a datum satisfying that equation with $T=0$ and $\\Lambda=0$;\nthis datum is such a solution, read as a model of the framework. The model is not locally flat: its curvature tensor is nowhere\nzero, while its Ricci tensor and Einstein tensor vanish identically\n([[lem-gr-schwarzschild-ricci-and-einstein-tensors]] clause (d)).\n\n**(b) Static clock readings.** The integral curves of $n$ are the static\nobserver worldlines at constant radius $r_0$\n([[lem-gr-schwarzschild-static-congruence-and-clock-rates]] clause (b));\nadopting the clock rule of [[post-gr-ideal-clocks-and-free-fall]] clause (i),\nthe reading difference of an ideal clock carried along such a worldline\nbetween two events whose coordinate times differ by $\\Delta t$ is\n\n$$\\Delta\\tau=\\sqrt{f(r_0)}\\,\\Delta t=\\sqrt{1-\\frac{s}{r_0}}\\;\\Delta t,$$\n\nso two static clocks at radii $r_1,r_2$ compared over the same coordinate\ninterval with $\\Delta t\\ne0$ satisfy $\\Delta\\tau_1/\\Delta\\tau_2=\\sqrt{f(r_1)/f(r_2)}$. The static\nworldlines are not geodesics: their acceleration is\n$a=\\frac{s}{2r^2}\\partial_r\\ne0$, so the model assigns them to accelerated\n(constrained) bodies and not to freely falling ones.\n\n**(c) The mass parameter.** The parameter $m_*$ enters the model only through\n$s=2G_Nm_*/c^2$ and through the quantities below; the framework reads $m_*$ as\nthe mass parameter of the model in kilograms\n([[def-gr-si-unit-and-coordinate-conventions]] clause (a),\n[[def-gr-schwarzschild-exterior-model]] clause (a)):\n\n1. Let $\\gamma:I\\to M$ be a smooth future timelike geodesic, parametrized\n   by proper time $\\tau$ on an interval with nonempty interior, with\n   $r\\circ\\gamma\\equiv r_0>\\frac{3s}{2}$. Put $\\sigma:=\\iota\\circ\\gamma:I\\to S^2$\n   and $t:=(x^0\\circ\\gamma)/c$. Its **angular speed with respect to $t$** is\n   $\\Omega:=\\sqrt{\\gamma_{S^2}(d\\sigma/dt,d\\sigma/dt)}$, where\n   $d\\sigma/dt:=\\sigma'(\\tau)/t'(\\tau)$ and $t'(\\tau)>0$ by future\n   timelikeness. It has units $\\mathrm s^{-1}$ and satisfies\n   $\\Omega^2=G_Nm_*/r_0^3$ in every angular orientation. For an equatorial\n   curve, $\\vartheta=\\pi/2$ and $\\Omega^2=(d\\varphi/dt)^2$ in local polar\n   charts ([[lem-gr-schwarzschild-circular-orbits-and-photon-sphere]]\n   clause (a) and proof step 2.2). This is the Kepler-like law in which\n   $m_*$ plays the role of the central mass;\n2. in the isotropic chart of\n   [[lem-gr-schwarzschild-isotropic-coordinate-form]], with\n   $a=s/(4\\rho)$, the exact identity\n   $g_{00}+1=1-\\bigl((1-a)/(1+a)\\bigr)^2=4a/(1+a)^2$ gives\n   $$g_{00}+1=+\\frac{s}{\\rho}+O(\\rho^{-2})=+\\frac{2G_Nm_*}{c^2\\rho}+O(\\rho^{-2}).$$\n   Under the weak-field convention $g_{00}=-(1+2\\Phi/c^2)$ to leading order\n   ([[def-gr-stationary-weak-field-data]]), this is the coefficient\n   $-2\\Phi_N/c^2$ for the explicit exterior Newtonian potential\n   $\\Phi_N(\\rho):=-G_Nm_*/\\rho$ in the comparison reading of\n   [[def-gr-newtonian-comparison-model-and-identification]].\n\nThe reading is a statement about the model's own identities and the adopted\nunit and weak-field conventions; no numerical value of $G_N$ or $m_*$, no\nNewtonian source model realized by matter and no comparison with any reported\nmass is asserted.\n\n**(d) Scope.** The model is an idealized vacuum model of the framework, not a\nclaim about the actual universe: no observation, apparatus, calibration or\nuncertainty is used, and no comparison with data is made. The datum solves the\nfield law with the chosen parameters $(m_*,\\Lambda,T)=(m_*,0,0)$; other\nsolutions of the same field law exist and are not excluded, and the exterior\ndomain $r>s$ contains no horizon or interior region, whose treatment is the\nsubject of the extension and Kruskal items of this page. The physical\ninterpretation of coordinates, clocks and bodies remains the adopted one of\nthe cited postulates.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "post-gr-ideal-clocks-and-free-fall",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Adopt as physical assumptions of the classical gravitational framework, in\naddition to [[post-gr-spacetime-and-classical-domain]] and the matter-content\nassumptions of the framework, the following rules by which ideal clocks, test\nbodies and light couple to the metric $g$ of\n[[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]].\n\n**(i) Ideal clocks.** An **ideal clock** is an idealized physical device\ncarried along a future timelike worldline ([[def-gr-timelike-worldline-and-proper-time]])\nwhose readings are assumed to reproduce the proper time of that worldline:\nthe difference of two readings taken at two events of the worldline is the\nproper-time increment between those events as defined by the metric, and it is\nindependent of the construction of the clock. Two ideal clocks moving along\nthe same worldline differ only by an additive constant (their choice of zero);\ndifferent worldlines generally accrue different proper times between the same\npair of events. The physical constant $c$ and the unit of duration are those\nof [[def-gr-si-unit-and-coordinate-conventions]].\n\n**(ii) Free fall of massive test bodies.** A **test body** is an uncharged\nidealized body whose internal structure is small compared with the scale over\nwhich the geometry of the model varies and whose gravitational self-field is\nnegligible within the model. An **ideal freely falling massive test body** is\nassumed to have a worldline that is a future timelike geodesic of the\nmetric-selected connection ([[def-gr-metric-selected-connection]],\n[[thm-gr-nondegenerate-levi-civita-and-tensor-derivative]],\n[[def-geodesic-of-an-affine-connection]]), traversed with physical\nfour-velocity $U=d\\gamma/d\\tau$ satisfying\n$g_{\\gamma(\\tau)}(U,U)=-c^2$; proper time is an affine parameter of the\nworldline ([[lem-gr-proper-time-reparametrization-and-four-velocity]]). The\nworldline is determined by the event at which the body is released together\nwith its four-velocity there, by the uniqueness of the geodesic initial-value\nproblem ([[thm-gr-local-geodesics-and-exponential-map]]); no force, charge, or\nrest mass of the body enters this rule.\n\n**(iii) Light rays.** In the geometric-optics approximation an **ideal light\nray** propagating in the model is assumed to be a future null geodesic of the\nmetric-selected connection\n([[def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors]],\n[[def-geodesic-of-an-affine-connection]]). A null worldline carries no proper\ntime and no four-velocity in the sense of\n[[def-gr-timelike-worldline-and-proper-time]]; within the approximation the\npropagation of an ideal ray is independent of its frequency, and only the\nray's direction in the tangent space is fixed, not a scale along it.\n\n**(iv) Scope and exclusions.** The three rules are adopted in the test-field\nregime of the smooth classical model: the clocks, bodies and rays do not\nback-react on the geometry, and the geometry varies smoothly on the scales\nrelevant to them. Configurations requiring the body's own gravitational field,\nradiation reaction, an electromagnetic or other self-force, finite-size or\ntidal couplings beyond the test-body idealization, non-geometric-optics\npropagation, dispersion or dissipation of light, or quantum effects are\noutside these assumptions and require additional model content. The\nassumptions do not assert that any real clock, body or light ray is exactly\nideal, nor do they attach measured readings to any model.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-gr-schwarzschild-static-congruence-and-clock-rates",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(M,g,N)$ be the Schwarzschild exterior datum of mass parameter $m_*$\nwith Schwarzschild radius $s>0$ and $f(r)=1-s/r$\n([[def-gr-schwarzschild-exterior-model]]), with metric-selected connection\n$\\nabla$ ([[def-gr-metric-selected-connection]],\n[[thm-gr-nondegenerate-levi-civita-and-tensor-derivative]]). Define the\nsmooth vector field\n\n$$n:=f^{-1/2}\\,\\partial_0=\\Bigl(1-\\frac{s}{r}\\Bigr)^{-1/2}\\partial_0$$\n\non $M$. Then:\n\n**(a) Observer congruence.** $n$ is a smooth observer congruence on $M$ in the\nsense of [[def-gr-observer-congruence-and-adapted-charts]]: $g(n,n)=-1$ and\n$g(n,N)<0$ at every point, so $n$ is a future-directed unit timelike field.\n\n**(b) Integral curves.** For any $p=(c_0,r_0,\\omega_0)\\in M$, including sphere poles, the maximal integral curve of $n$ through $p$, with length-valued flow parameter $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R$, is\n$$\\lambda\\mapsto\\bigl(c_0+f(r_0)^{-1/2}\\lambda,\\ r_0,\\ \\omega_0\\bigr).$$\nIt is defined on all of $\\mathbb R$ and is the **static observer worldline** at radius $r_0$. In a spherical-polar chart it has constant angular coordinates $(\\vartheta_0,\\varphi_0)$; in any angular chart the sphere point is constant, and the global coordinate $x^0$ increases monotonically.\n\n**(c) Proper time and clock rate.** Along each static observer worldline the\nproper time $\\tau$ is an affine function of the flow parameter $\\lambda$, with\n$\\frac{d\\tau}{d\\lambda}=\\frac1c$, and\n\n$$\\Delta\\tau=\\frac{\\sqrt{f(r_0)}}{c}\\,\\Delta x^0=\\sqrt{f(r_0)}\\,\\Delta t,$$\n\nwhere $\\Delta x^0$ is the coordinate difference in metres between two events\nof the worldline and $\\Delta t=\\Delta x^0/c$ is the same difference in\nseconds. In particular the proper-time increment is strictly positive for\n$\\Delta x^0>0$, and it is smaller than $\\Delta t$ for $\\Delta x^0>0$ at every finite radius\nbecause $0<f(r_0)<1$.\n\n**(d) Clock-rate ratio.** For two static observers at radii\n$r_1,r_2\\in(s,\\infty)$ compared over the same coordinate interval\n$\\Delta x^0\\ne0$, the proper-time increments satisfy\n\n$$\\frac{\\Delta\\tau_1}{\\Delta\\tau_2}=\\sqrt{\\frac{f(r_1)}{f(r_2)}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{1-s/r_1}{1-s/r_2}} .$$\n\n**(e) Acceleration.** The acceleration field of the congruence is\n$a:=\\nabla_nn=\\frac{f'(r)}{2}\\,\\partial_1=\\frac{s}{2r^2}\\,\\partial_r$; it is\nspatial, nowhere zero, and points in the direction of increasing $r$, so the\nstatic observers are not geodesics.\n\n**(f) Scope.** These are identities of the geometric datum. The reading of\n$\\tau$ as the time measured by an ideal clock and of the congruence as a\nfamily of static observers belongs to the framework's postulates and is not\nasserted here; no measurement and no comparison with any observed rate is\nmade.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "pthm-gr-schwarzschild-stationary-redshift",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Adopt the Schwarzschild exterior vacuum model of\n[[pthm-gr-schwarzschild-vacuum-model]]: the exterior datum $(M,g,N)$ of mass\nparameter $m_*>0$ with $s=2G_Nm_*/c^2$ and $f(r)=1-s/r$\n([[def-gr-schwarzschild-exterior-model]]), its metric-selected connection\n$\\nabla$ ([[def-gr-metric-selected-connection]],\n[[thm-gr-nondegenerate-levi-civita-and-tensor-derivative]]), its static\ncongruence $n=f^{-1/2}\\partial_0$\n([[lem-gr-schwarzschild-static-congruence-and-clock-rates]]), and the light-ray\nrule that ideal light rays are future null geodesics of $\\nabla$\n([[post-gr-ideal-clocks-and-free-fall]] clause (iii)). Let $\\eta:J\\to M$ be\nthe specified ray, a smooth affinely parametrized geodesic of $\\nabla$ on an\ninterval $J$, with tangent $W=\\eta'$, $g(W,W)=0$ and $W$ future-directed\n([[def-geodesic-of-an-affine-connection]],\n[[def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors]]). Let\n$\\lambda_E,\\lambda_R\\in J$ with $\\lambda_E<\\lambda_R$ and suppose\n$\\eta(\\lambda_E)$ and $\\eta(\\lambda_R)$ lie on static observer worldlines of\nradii $r_E=r(\\eta(\\lambda_E))$ and $r_R=r(\\eta(\\lambda_R))$ with\n$r_E,r_R\\in(s,\\infty)$. For each parameter value define the **observer energy\nof the ray relative to the static observers** by\n\n$$E(\\lambda):=-c\\,g_{\\eta(\\lambda)}\\bigl(W(\\lambda),n(\\eta(\\lambda))\\bigr),$$\n\nthe value of the observer-energy definition of\n[[def-gr-observer-relative-energy-and-momentum]] clause (a) for the unit\nobserver $n$. Then:\n\n**(a) Constancy of the Killing pairing.** $K=\\partial_0$ is a Killing field of\n$g$ and the function $C(\\lambda):=-g_{\\eta(\\lambda)}(W(\\lambda),K(\\eta(\\lambda)))$\nis constant along $J$;\nmoreover $C>0$.\n\n**(b) The observer-energy ratio.** $E(\\lambda)>0$ along the ray and\n\n$$\\frac{E(\\lambda_R)}{E(\\lambda_E)}=\\sqrt{\\frac{f(r_E)}{f(r_R)}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{1-s/r_E}{1-s/r_R}} .$$\n\n**(c) Redshift parameter.** If the **redshift parameter** of the pair of events\nis defined by $z:=\\dfrac{E(\\lambda_E)}{E(\\lambda_R)}-1$, equivalently by\n$1+z=E(\\lambda_E)/E(\\lambda_R)$, then\n\n$$1+z=\\sqrt{\\frac{f(r_R)}{f(r_E)}} .$$\n\nThe ratio in (b) and (c) is unchanged if $W$ is replaced by $\\mu W$ with a\npositive constant $\\mu$: a future null ray determines its future tangent only\nup to positive scale,\nand only the ratio of energies is asserted. No observed wavelength, spectral\nline, calibration or measurement enters the statement; the identification of\nthe ratio with a frequency ratio of an ideal ray is part of the framework's\ngeometric-optics reading, and moving observers require additional factors.\n\n**(d) Scope.** The statement is a model prediction for the specified ideal\nray and the specified static observers; it asserts no redshift measurement and\nno property of any real source, and it excludes non-geometric-optics\npropagation, dispersion and any apparatus or analysis model.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-gr-schwarzschild-exterior-model",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "This item assumes the axiom of countable choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ through\nthe tangent-bundle structure used by\n[[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]] clause (c)\n([[def-countable-choice]]); no further choice principle is used.\n\n**(a) Parameters and the Schwarzschild radius.** Let $c>0$ and $G_N>0$ be real\nconstants and let $m_*>0$ be a real **mass parameter**. Put\n\n$$s:=\\frac{2G_Nm_*}{c^2}>0 .$$\n\nThe number $s$ is the **Schwarzschild radius** of the datum. Neither $c$,\n$G_N$ nor $m_*$ is measured here; the physical reading of $c$ and $G_N$ as the\nframework's conversion and Newton constants and of $m_*$ as a mass is fixed by\nthe physical conventions of the framework, not by this definition.\n\n**(b) The underlying manifold and its product charts.** Let $S^2:=S_2(0,1)=\n\\{y\\in\\mathbb R^3:\\lVert y\\rVert_2=1\\}$ be the unit Euclidean sphere with the\nsubspace topology of [[def-euclidean-spheres-and-closed-balls]]. Specify its\nsmooth structure as the maximal atlas generated by the following two charts\n([[def-smooth-manifold]]). For $\\varepsilon\\in\\{+1,-1\\}$ put\n$p_\\varepsilon=(0,0,\\varepsilon)$ and\n$U_\\varepsilon=S^2\\setminus\\{p_\\varepsilon\\}$, and define\n\n$$\\sigma_\\varepsilon:U_\\varepsilon\\to\\mathbb R^2,\\qquad \\sigma_\\varepsilon(y)=\\frac{(y_1,y_2)}{1-\\varepsilon y_3}.$$\n\nThese open domains cover $S^2$. Each chart is a homeomorphism onto\n$\\mathbb R^2$, with inverse, for $u=(u_1,u_2)$ and $q=u_1^2+u_2^2$,\n\n$$\\sigma_\\varepsilon^{-1}(u)=\\left(\\frac{2u_1}{1+q},\\frac{2u_2}{1+q},\\varepsilon\\frac{q-1}{1+q}\\right).$$\n\nThe two inverse identities follow by substitution; all displayed denominators\nare nonzero on their domains. On the overlap both transition maps are\n$u\\mapsto u/(u_1^2+u_2^2)$ on $\\mathbb R^2\\setminus\\{0\\}$, hence smooth.\nThe subspace is Hausdorff and has a countable basis given by intersections\nwith Euclidean balls of rational centres and positive rational radii.\nThus these charts specify a smooth two-manifold without boundary. This is\nthe standard sphere structure used for the round metric below: the inclusion\nhas the displayed smooth local parametrizations, and their differentials\nare injective because composition with $\\sigma_\\varepsilon$ is the identity.\nLet $M$ be the product manifold\n\n$$M:=\\mathbb R\\times(s,\\infty)\\times S^2$$\n\nwith the product smooth structure\n([[prop-products-of-smooth-manifolds-have-a-canonical-product-smooth-structure]]);\nit is a smooth four-manifold without boundary. Write $x^0:M\\to\\mathbb R$ and\n$r:M\\to(s,\\infty)$ for the first and second projections and $\\iota:M\\to S^2$\nfor the third. A **product chart** of $M$ is a smooth chart on the product\nwhose angular part is a smooth chart of $S^2$. In a **spherical-polar product\nchart**, $0<\\vartheta<\\pi$ and the components of the round metric\n$\\gamma_{S^2}$ of [[ex-the-round-metric-on-the-sphere-as-an-induced-metric]]\nare $\\gamma_{22}=1$, $\\gamma_{23}=\\gamma_{32}=0$,\n$\\gamma_{33}=\\sin^2\\vartheta$. These polar charts omit the two poles; ordinary\nsmooth sphere charts around the poles complete the product atlas.\n\n**(c) The metric.** Let $f:(s,\\infty)\\to(0,\\infty)$ be the smooth function\n\n$$f(r):=1-\\frac{s}{r} .$$\n\nDefine $g$ globally by the coordinate-free expression\n\n$$g=-f\\,d(x^0)\\otimes d(x^0)+f^{-1}\\,dr\\otimes dr+r^2\\,\\iota^*\\gamma_{S^2}.$$\n\nIn every spherical-polar product chart its component functions are\n\n$$g_{00}:=-f(r),\\qquad g_{11}:=\\frac{1}{f(r)},\\qquad g_{22}:=r^2,\\qquad g_{33}:=r^2\\sin^2\\vartheta,$$\n\nand $g_{ab}:=0$ whenever $a\\ne b$, $a,b\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}$, with the index order\n$(x^0,x^1,x^2,x^3)=(x^0,r,\\vartheta,\\varphi)$. This is a smooth symmetric\ncovariant $2$-tensor field on all of $M$\n([[def-gr-smooth-covariant-two-tensor-field]]); the component display is local\nto spherical-polar charts, and $f>0$ because $r>s$. The pair $(M,g)$ is the **Schwarzschild exterior\nmetric of mass parameter $m_*$**; the datum of this definition is the\nquadruple $(c,G_N,m_*,g)$. With these parameters fixed, write $(M,g,N)$\nfor its associated geometric datum, with $N$ as in clause (d). The geometric\ndatum depends on the parameters only through $s$ and does not retain $c$,\n$G_N$ and $m_*$ separately. That $(M,g,N)$ is a time-oriented smooth Lorentzian four-manifold\nof signature $(-,+,+,+)$ is not asserted here; it is proved in the companion metric-properties lemma of this page (its\njustification record in the frontmatter), which also records the equivalent\ncoordinate-free description\n$g=-f\\,d(x^0)\\otimes d(x^0)+f^{-1}\\,dr\\otimes dr+r^2\\,\\iota^*\\gamma_{S^2}$.\n\n**(d) Time orientation and the Schwarzschild chart.** The field\n$N:=\\partial_0$ is the smooth product vector field on $M$\n([[def-derivation-at-a-point-and-tangent-space]]); the chart components of $g$\nare independent of $x^0$, so $N$ is the restriction of a global vector field,\nand $g(N,N)=-f(r)<0$ at every point. The datum carries the time orientation\n$N$. Spherical-polar product charts are **Schwarzschild charts** of the datum;\nat the poles the same metric is represented in the smooth sphere charts of\nthe product atlas. We write $t$ for the coordinate $x^0/c$ when the datum is\nread in the framework's unit conventions.\n\n**(e) Scope.** The domain of the datum is the exterior region $r>s$; the\ndefinition asserts nothing about $r=s$, about $r\\le 0$, about any matter, or\nabout any equation satisfied by $g$. The horizon $r=s$ is not part of the\ndatum, and a continuation of the metric to $r=s$ requires the separate constructions recorded on this page, the\nregularity lemma for the advanced chart and the Kruskal model-properties\nlemma. Whether the datum is a solution of the\nframework's field law is proved, not defined, in the Ricci-and-Einstein-tensor lemma of this\npage.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-integral-logarithm",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $x>0$, define the **integral logarithm**\n\n$$L(x):=\\int_1^x\\frac{dt}{t},$$\n\nusing the oriented integral when $x<1$\n([[def-oriented-integral]]).\n\nThis is well defined. The function $t\\mapsto1/t$ is continuous wherever\n$t\\neq0$ by the quotient clause of\n[[thm-algebra-of-continuous-functions]]. For $x\\neq1$ it is therefore continuous on the nondegenerate compact interval\nwith endpoints $1$ and $x$, hence Riemann integrable there by\n[[thm-continuous-implies-integrable]], whose hypothesis is $a<b$. At $x=1$ the\ninterval is degenerate and that theorem does not apply; there\n[[def-oriented-integral]] stipulates $\\int_1^1f=0$, so $L(1)=0$ directly.",
          "uses": [
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-ftc-first-part",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a < b$ be reals, let $f : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be integrable\n([[def-darboux-integral]]), let $F$ be its integral function\n([[def-the-integral-function]]), and let $c \\in [a,b]$ be a point at which $f$ is\ncontinuous ([[def-continuity-real]]). Then $F$ is differentiable at $c$ as a\nfunction on $[a,b]$ ([[def-derivative]]) and\n\n$$F'(c) \\;=\\; f(c) .$$\n\nAt $c = a$ and $c = b$ this is the one-sided statement, which is what\n[[def-derivative]] means at those points: every point of a nondegenerate interval\nis a limit point of it, so $F'(c)$ is a meaningful symbol at every $c \\in [a,b]$,\nand the difference quotient is taken over $[a,b] \\setminus \\{c\\}$.\n\n**Consequently, if $f$ is continuous on the whole of $[a,b]$, then $F$ is a\nprimitive of $f$ there**: $F' = f$ at every point of $[a,b]$.\n\n**Continuity at $c$ is a hypothesis and it cannot be dropped.** For an integrable\n$f$ that is discontinuous at $c$, $F'(c)$ may fail to exist, and it may exist and\ndiffer from $f(c)$; both are exhibited on the companion page, by\nan integrable function with no primitive and by a false\nstatement about the integral function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-monotonicity-of-the-integral",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a < b$ be reals and let $f, g : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be integrable\n([[def-darboux-integral]]). Then:\n\n1. **Nonnegativity.** If $f(x) \\ge 0$ for every $x \\in [a,b]$ then\n   $\\int_a^b f \\ge 0$.\n2. **Monotonicity.** If $f(x) \\le g(x)$ for every $x \\in [a,b]$ then\n   $$\\int_a^b f \\;\\le\\; \\int_a^b g .$$\n3. **Two-sided bound.** If $m \\le f(x) \\le M$ for every $x \\in [a,b]$, with\n   $m, M$ real, then\n   $$m\\,(b-a) \\;\\le\\; \\int_a^b f \\;\\le\\; M\\,(b-a) .$$\n\n**Equality in claim 1 does not force $f$ to vanish.** A nonnegative integrable\nfunction with integral $0$ may be positive at infinitely many points; that is\n[[fs-nonnegative-integrable-with-zero-integral-vanishes]] on the previous page's\ncompanion. Under the additional hypothesis of continuity the conclusion does\nhold, and that is [[thm-nonnegative-continuous-with-zero-integral-vanishes]]\nbelow.\n\n**Claim 2 is stated for $a < b$ and is not orientation-invariant.** With the\nconvention of [[def-oriented-integral]], $f \\le g$ gives\n$\\int_u^v f \\le \\int_u^v g$ when $u \\le v$ and the reverse inequality when\n$u \\ge v$, since both sides change sign together.",
          "uses": [
            "1.3"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "By [F1], $U=cn$ is the static observer four-velocity because $g(U,U)=-c^2$. Since $c$ is constant, $\\nabla_UU=c^2\\nabla_nn=c^2s\\partial_r/(2r^2)$. With $g(\\partial_r,\\partial_r)=f^{-1}$ from [F3], its squared norm is $c^4s^2/(4r^4f)$, so its positive magnitude is $c^2s/(2r^2\\sqrt f)$. Substituting $s=2G_Nm_*/c^2$ gives the displayed Newton-constant form.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "Over the same coordinate-time interval, [F1] gives $\\Delta\\tau_R/\\Delta\\tau_E=\\sqrt{f(r_R)/f(r_E)}$. [F2] gives the inverse ratio for received to emitted ideal-ray frequency, so the two displayed exact ratios follow. Each ratio is a conditional model result for the specified static observers and ideal ray.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-3",
          "claim": "For $0<x\\le\\rho<1$, the chain rule and [F4] show that $d\\log(1-x)/dx=-1/(1-x)=-1-x/(1-x)$. Integrating from $0$ to $x$, using $\\log(1)=0$, gives $\\log(1-x)=-x-\\int_0^x\\frac{t}{1-t}\\,dt,\\qquad 0\\le\\int_0^x\\frac{t}{1-t}\\,dt\\le\\frac{1}{1-\\rho}\\int_0^x t\\,dt=\\frac{x^2}{2(1-\\rho)}.$ For $t\\in[0, x]$, $0\\le t/(1-t)\\le t/(1-\\rho)$. Thus [F5] and $\\int_0^x t\\,dt=x^2/2$ give the displayed bound. By [F2], $\\log(\\omega_R/\\omega_E)=\\frac12[\\log(1-x_E)-\\log(1-x_R)]$. Inserting the integral identity separates the linear term $(x_R-x_E)/2=s(1/r_R-1/r_E)/2$. The remaining terms have absolute value at most half the sum of their nonnegative integral bounds, namely $(x_E^2+x_R^2)/(4(1-\\rho))$.",
          "step": "1.3",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The exact ratios, acceleration magnitude and uniform remainder bound are all consequences of the stated model identities; no empirical input is used.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 1.3",
            "A1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The statement assumes s>0 and radii r_E,r_R>s, so both static observer domains are nonempty."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The statement has x_i>0; for rho=0 there are no finite admissible radii, and step 1.3 gives zero remainder when r_E=r_R."
