    "lem-rpm-sine-over-angle-limit": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The functions $\\sin$ and $\\cos$ are differentiable on $\\mathbb R$, with\n$$(\\sin x)'=\\cos x,\\qquad(\\cos x)'=-\\sin x.$$\nAlso $\\sin0=0$ and $\\cos0=1$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "cor-differentiable-implies-continuous",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $A \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$, let $f : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ and let $c \\in A$ be a\nlimit point of $A$ ([[def-limit-point-r]]). If $f$ is differentiable at $c$\n([[def-derivative]]) then $f$ is continuous at $c$ ([[def-continuity-real]]).\n\nConsequently, if $f$ is differentiable on a set $S \\subseteq A$ then $f$ is\ncontinuous at every point of $S$.\n\n**No converse is asserted, and none holds.** Continuity at $c$ does not give\ndifferentiability at $c$, and the standard witness is worked out on the\ncompanion page.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "cor-mean-value-theorem",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a, b \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $a < b$ and let $f : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be\ncontinuous on $[a,b]$ ([[def-continuity-real]], [[def-interval]]) and\ndifferentiable at every point of $(a,b)$ as a function on $[a,b]$\n([[def-derivative]]). Then there is $c \\in (a,b)$ with\n\n$$f(b) - f(a) \\;=\\; f'(c)\\,(b - a) .$$\n\nEquivalently, since $b - a \\ne 0$, there is $c \\in (a,b)$ at which\n$f'(c) = \\bigl(f(b)-f(a)\\bigr)/(b-a)$: the derivative somewhere inside equals\nthe average rate of change across the whole interval.\n\n**Continuity on the closed interval cannot be dropped.** Differentiability at\nevery point of $(a,b)$ alone does not suffice: a function on $[0,1]$,\ndifferentiable at every point of $(0,1)$ with derivative constantly $1$, for\nwhich no $c$ works, is exhibited later on this page as a false statement, and\nthe companion page works the same witness out in full.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Fix $x\\ne0$. By [F1] and [F2], sine is continuous on the closed interval with endpoints $0,x$ and differentiable in its interior. By [F3] there is $\\xi_x$ strictly between $0$ and $x$ such that $\\sin x/x=(\\sin x-\\sin0)/(x-0)=\\cos\\xi_x$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Given $\\varepsilon>0$, [F1] and [F2] make cosine continuous at $0$, so choose $\\delta>0$ such that $|t|<\\delta$ implies $|\\cos t-1|<\\varepsilon$. If $0<|x|<\\delta$, then $|\\xi_x|<|x|<\\delta$ and step 1.1 yields $|\\sin x/x-1|=|\\cos\\xi_x-1|<\\varepsilon$. This is the stated limit.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The real interval around zero is nonempty; step 1.1 applies to every nonzero x in it."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 2.1 proves the limit as x approaches zero while the quotient is defined only for x nonzero."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The statement has no one-element, unit-object, or single-component normalization case."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The statement has no separate degenerate parameter branch."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 1.1 applies the mean value theorem on the interval between 0 and x for either sign of x."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Steps 1.1–2.1 apply the mean value theorem separately for each x and make no simultaneous selection; no choice axiom is used."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The statement is not an if-and-only-if claim, so there is no forward direction to verify."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The statement is not an if-and-only-if claim, so there is no reverse direction to verify."
        }
      ],
      "finite_checks": []
    },
    "lem-rpm-arctangent-reciprocal-angle": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-principal-inverse-tangent",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "By [[lem-tangent-principal-branch-is-bijective]], tangent restricts to a\ncontinuous strictly increasing bijection\n\n$$\\tan:(-\\pi/2,\\pi/2)\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R.$$\n\nIts inverse is the **principal inverse tangent**\n\n$$\\arctan:\\mathbb R\\longrightarrow(-\\pi/2,\\pi/2).$$\n\nThus $\\tan(\\arctan y)=y$ for every real $y$, while\n$\\arctan(\\tan x)=x$ precisely for $x$ in the displayed principal interval.\nThe inverse is continuous and strictly increasing by [[thm-continuous-inverse]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-tangent-principal-branch-is-bijective",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The restriction\n\n$$\\tan:(-\\pi/2,\\pi/2)\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R$$\n\nis continuous, strictly increasing, and bijective.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-tangent-cotangent-secant-cosecant",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Define\n$$\\tan x:=\\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}\\quad(x\\ne\\pi/2+m\\pi),\\qquad\\cot x:=\\frac{\\cos x}{\\sin x}\\quad(x\\ne m\\pi),$$\n$$\\sec x:=\\frac1{\\cos x}\\quad(x\\ne\\pi/2+m\\pi),\\qquad\\csc x:=\\frac1{\\sin x}\\quad(x\\ne m\\pi),$$\nwhere $m$ ranges over the integers. The exclusions are exactly the zero sets of [[thm-sine-cosine-zero-sets-and-fundamental-period]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The functions $\\sin$ and $\\cos$ are differentiable on $\\mathbb R$, with\n$$(\\sin x)'=\\cos x,\\qquad(\\cos x)'=-\\sin x.$$\nAlso $\\sin0=0$ and $\\cos0=1$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-quarter-turn-values-and-shift-formulas",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every real $x$,\n$$\\sin(x+\\pi/2)=\\cos x,\\quad\\cos(x+\\pi/2)=-\\sin x,\\quad\\sin(x+\\pi)=-\\sin x,\\quad\\cos(x+\\pi)=-\\cos x.$$\nIn particular,\n$$\\sin(\\pi/2)=1,\\quad\\cos(\\pi/2)=0,\\quad\\sin\\pi=0,\\quad\\cos\\pi=-1.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-pythagorean-and-parity-identities-for-all-six-trigonometric-functions",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every real $x$, $\\sin ^2x+\\cos ^2x=1$, $\\sin(-x)=-\\sin x$, and $\\cos(-x)=\\cos x$. Wherever the displayed quotients are defined, $\\tan(-x)=-\\tan x$, $\\cot(-x)=-\\cot x$, $\\sec(-x)=\\sec x$, $\\csc(-x)=-\\csc x$, $1+\\tan^2x=\\sec^2x$, and $1+\\cot^2x=\\csc^2x$. The conventions and prerequisite facts used below are recorded in [[cor-trigonometric-parity-and-pythagorean-identity]], [[def-tangent-cotangent-secant-cosecant]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-sine-cosine-signs-monotonicity-and-ranges",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Sine is strictly increasing on each interval $[-\\pi/2+2m\\pi,\\pi/2+2m\\pi]$ and strictly decreasing on each interval $[\\pi/2+2m\\pi,3\\pi/2+2m\\pi]$. Cosine is strictly decreasing on $[2m\\pi,(2m+1)\\pi]$ and strictly increasing on $[(2m+1)\\pi,(2m+2)\\pi]$. Both functions have range $[-1,1]$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-tangent-cotangent-secant-cosecant",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Define\n$$\\tan x:=\\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}\\quad(x\\ne\\pi/2+m\\pi),\\qquad\\cot x:=\\frac{\\cos x}{\\sin x}\\quad(x\\ne m\\pi),$$\n$$\\sec x:=\\frac1{\\cos x}\\quad(x\\ne\\pi/2+m\\pi),\\qquad\\csc x:=\\frac1{\\sin x}\\quad(x\\ne m\\pi),$$\nwhere $m$ ranges over the integers. The exclusions are exactly the zero sets of [[thm-sine-cosine-zero-sets-and-fundamental-period]].",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Put $u=\\arctan y$. By [F3], $\\tan0=0$; [F1] therefore gives $\\arctan0=0$. Since $y>0$ and the inverse in [F1] is strictly increasing, $u>0$, while its range gives $u<\\pi/2$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The angle $\\pi/2-u$ lies in $(0,\\pi/2)$. The shift and parity identities in [F3] give $\\sin(\\pi/2-u)=\\cos u$ and $\\cos(\\pi/2-u)=\\sin u$. By [F4], $\\cos u>0$; because $\\tan u=y>0$ and $\\tan u=\\sin u/\\cos u$, also $\\sin u>0$. Thus tangent is defined at both $u$ and $\\pi/2-u$, and [F2] gives $\\tan(\\pi/2-u)=\\cos u/\\sin u=1/y$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Since $\\pi/2-u\\in(-\\pi/2,\\pi/2)$, the inverse identity in [F1] yields $\\arctan(1/y)=\\pi/2-u$. Substituting $u=\\arctan y$ proves the formula.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "F1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
