    "prop-rpm-relativistic-coulomb-scattering-branch": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-rpm-relativistic-coulomb-phase-system",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $m,c,\\kappa>0$, let $x,p\\in\\mathbb R^3$, and set\n\n$$D:=(\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{0\\})\\times\\mathbb R^3,\\qquad r:=|x|,\\qquad \\mathcal E(p):=\\sqrt{m^2c^4+c^2|p|^2}.$$\n\nThe **repulsive relativistic Coulomb Hamiltonian** is\n\n$$H(x,p):=\\mathcal E(p)+\\frac{\\kappa}{r},$$\n\nand its **phase system** is the autonomous ODE on the open set $D$\n\n$$\\dot x=\\nabla_pH=\\frac{c^2p}{\\mathcal E(p)},\\qquad \\dot p=-\\nabla_xH=\\frac{\\kappa x}{r^3}.$$\n\nHere the system parameter $t$ is time in seconds and dot denotes $d/dt$; $m$ is a positive mass parameter (kilograms in the SI interpretation), $c$ a positive speed parameter (metres per second), and $\\kappa$ a positive energy-times-length parameter (joule-metres). Define the radial momentum $p_r:=x\\cdot p/r$ and angular-momentum vector $J:=x\\times p$. A trajectory is a $C^1$ solution of this ODE on an interval; maximality is in the sense of [[def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution]].\n\nThis is only a mathematical phase system. Its physical interpretation as a charged particle in an externally prescribed Coulomb field requires separate model assumptions.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1",
            "3.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-picard-lindelof-local-existence-and-uniqueness",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $D\\subseteq\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^n$ be open, let $F:D\\to\\mathbb R^n$ be continuous and locally Lipschitz in the state variable, and let $(t_0,x_0)\\in D$. Then a unique local solution of the IVP exists on some interval around the initial time. More quantitatively, if $h,r>0$ make\n\n$$[t_0-h,t_0+h]\\times\\overline B(x_0,r)\\subseteq D,$$\n\nif $\\|F\\|_2\\le M$ and $F$ has state-Lipschitz constant $L$ on this cylinder, and if $hM\\le r$ and $Lh<1$, then there is exactly one solution on $[t_0-h,t_0+h]$ through $(t_0,x_0)$ whose graph lies in the cylinder. Any two solutions through the same initial data agree on every common subinterval containing $t_0$.\n\nIn particular, a unique local solution exists on an interval around the initial time.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-cm-compact-continuation-for-locally-lipschitz-odes",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n\\ge1$, let $D\\subseteq\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^n$ be open, and let $F:D\\to\\mathbb R^n$ be continuous and locally Lipschitz in the state variable, locally uniformly in time, in the sense of [[def-locally-lipschitz-in-the-state-variable]]. Let $t_0<b$ be reals, let $x_0\\in\\mathbb R^n$ with $(t_0,x_0)\\in D$, and let $x:[t_0,b)\\to\\mathbb R^n$ be a solution of the initial value problem\n\n$$x'(t)=F\\bigl(t,x(t)\\bigr),\\qquad x(t_0)=x_0$$\n\non $[t_0,b)$ ([[def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution]]). Suppose there is a compact set $K\\subseteq D$ and a real $\\delta>0$ such that\n\n$$\\bigl(t,x(t)\\bigr)\\in K\\qquad\\text{for every }t\\in(b-\\delta,b)\\cap[t_0,b).$$\n\nThen:\n\n1. there is $\\varepsilon>0$ and a solution $\\tilde x:[t_0,b+\\varepsilon)\\to\\mathbb R^n$ of the same initial value problem with $\\tilde x(t)=x(t)$ for all $t\\in[t_0,b)$; in particular $\\tilde x(b)=\\lim_{t\\uparrow b}x(t)$ exists and lies in the state projection of $K$;\n2. if moreover $D=\\mathbb R\\times U$ for an open $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ and $F$ is autonomous, $F(t,z)=G(z)$ with $G:U\\to\\mathbb R^n$ continuous and locally Lipschitz in the state variable ([[def-locally-lipschitz-in-the-state-variable]]), and if there is a compact $K_0\\subseteq U$ with $x(t)\\in K_0$ for every $t\\in(b-\\delta,b)\\cap[t_0,b)$, then the extension conclusion of clause 1 holds as well.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-principal-inverse-tangent",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "By [[lem-tangent-principal-branch-is-bijective]], tangent restricts to a\ncontinuous strictly increasing bijection\n\n$$\\tan:(-\\pi/2,\\pi/2)\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R.$$\n\nIts inverse is the **principal inverse tangent**\n\n$$\\arctan:\\mathbb R\\longrightarrow(-\\pi/2,\\pi/2).$$\n\nThus $\\tan(\\arctan y)=y$ for every real $y$, while\n$\\arctan(\\tan x)=x$ precisely for $x$ in the displayed principal interval.\nThe inverse is continuous and strictly increasing by [[thm-continuous-inverse]].",
          "uses": [
            "3.2",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-tangent-principal-branch-is-bijective",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The restriction\n\n$$\\tan:(-\\pi/2,\\pi/2)\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R$$\n\nis continuous, strictly increasing, and bijective.",
          "uses": [
            "3.2",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-principal-inverse-sine-and-cosine",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Sine is differentiable, hence continuous, and strictly increasing on\n$[-\\pi/2,\\pi/2]$; its endpoint values are $-1$ and $1$\n([[thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives]],\n[[cor-differentiable-implies-continuous]],\n[[thm-sine-cosine-signs-monotonicity-and-ranges]],\n[[thm-quarter-turn-values-and-shift-formulas]],\n[[cor-trigonometric-parity-and-pythagorean-identity]]). The intermediate value\ntheorem therefore makes its restricted image exactly $[-1,1]$\n([[thm-intermediate-value]]). Likewise, cosine is continuous and strictly\ndecreasing on $[0,\\pi]$, with endpoint values $1$ and $-1$, so its restricted\nimage is $[-1,1]$ ([[thm-sine-cosine-signs-monotonicity-and-ranges]],\n[[thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives]], [[cor-differentiable-implies-continuous]],\n[[thm-quarter-turn-values-and-shift-formulas]], [[thm-intermediate-value]]).\nTheir principal inverses are denoted\n\n$$\\arcsin:[-1,1]\\to[-\\pi/2,\\pi/2],\\qquad\\arccos:[-1,1]\\to[0,\\pi],$$\n\nand are characterised by\n\n$$\\sin(\\arcsin y)=y,\\qquad\\cos(\\arccos y)=y\\qquad(-1\\le y\\le1).$$\n\nThe chosen target intervals are part of the notation: without them, inverse\nsine and inverse cosine would be multivalued relations rather than functions.\nTheir continuity follows from [[thm-continuous-inverse]].",
          "uses": [
            "3.2",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-derivative-of-an-inverse",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $I \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ be order-convex with at least two elements\n([[def-interval]]), let $f : I \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be continuous on $I$\n([[def-continuity-real]]) and injective\n([[def-injection-surjection-bijection]]), and let $g : f[I] \\to I$ be the\ninverse of $f : I \\to f[I]$ supplied by [[thm-continuous-inverse]]. Let\n$c \\in I$ and put $b := f(c)$.\n\nThen $c$ is a limit point of $I$ and $b$ is a limit point of $f[I]$, so that\n$f'(c)$ and $g'(b)$ are meaningful symbols ([[def-derivative]]), and, assuming\n$f$ is differentiable at $c$:\n\n1. if $f'(c) \\ne 0$, then $g$ is differentiable at $b$ and\n   $$g'(b) \\;=\\; \\frac{1}{f'(c)} ;$$\n2. if $f'(c) = 0$, then $g$ is **not** differentiable at $b$.\n\n**The two claims together say that the inverse inherits differentiability\nexactly where the derivative does not vanish.** Nothing is asserted at a point\nof $f[I]$ that is not of the form $f(c)$ with $f$ differentiable at $c$, and\nnothing is asserted about $g$ being differentiable *on* a set.\n\n**No compactness and no boundedness is assumed.** $I$ may be open, half-open or\nunbounded; all that is used of it is order-convexity and the presence of two\ndistinct points, the latter being exactly what makes every point of $I$ a limit\npoint of $I$ ([[def-derivative]]).",
          "uses": [
            "3.2",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-sine-cosine-signs-monotonicity-and-ranges",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Sine is strictly increasing on each interval $[-\\pi/2+2m\\pi,\\pi/2+2m\\pi]$ and strictly decreasing on each interval $[\\pi/2+2m\\pi,3\\pi/2+2m\\pi]$. Cosine is strictly decreasing on $[2m\\pi,(2m+1)\\pi]$ and strictly increasing on $[(2m+1)\\pi,(2m+2)\\pi]$. Both functions have range $[-1,1]$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.2",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The functions $\\sin$ and $\\cos$ are differentiable on $\\mathbb R$, with\n$$(\\sin x)'=\\cos x,\\qquad(\\cos x)'=-\\sin x.$$\nAlso $\\sin0=0$ and $\\cos0=1$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.2",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-pythagorean-and-parity-identities-for-all-six-trigonometric-functions",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every real $x$, $\\sin ^2x+\\cos ^2x=1$, $\\sin(-x)=-\\sin x$, and $\\cos(-x)=\\cos x$. Wherever the displayed quotients are defined, $\\tan(-x)=-\\tan x$, $\\cot(-x)=-\\cot x$, $\\sec(-x)=\\sec x$, $\\csc(-x)=-\\csc x$, $1+\\tan^2x=\\sec^2x$, and $1+\\cot^2x=\\csc^2x$. The conventions and prerequisite facts used below are recorded in [[cor-trigonometric-parity-and-pythagorean-identity]], [[def-tangent-cotangent-secant-cosecant]].",
          "uses": [
