    "pthm-rpm-relativistic-coulomb-scattering": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-rpm-charge-spin-model",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Use the smooth, time-oriented Lorentzian background $(M,g,N)$, signature $(-,+,+,+)$, and the distinct chart, observer, congruence, tetrad and worldline meanings of [[def-rpm-spacetime-observer-and-worldline]]. The metric and time orientation are prescribed data, not dynamical variables. A **charged spinning test body** in this definition consists of the following model quantities along a massive worldline $z:I\\to M$:\n\n- $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ is an open interval whose parameter is proper time $\\tau$ in seconds; $z$ is $C^2$, future-directed and timelike, and $U:=dz/d\\tau$ obeys $g(U,U)=-c^2$. The SI conversion constant is $c>0$ metres per second; in a flat specialization use the affine Minkowski model and the proper-time decomposition of [[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]] and [[thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition]].\n- The constant rest-mass parameter $m>0$ has unit kilogram, and the constant charge parameter $q\\in\\mathbb R$ has unit coulomb. They are primitive model parameters; this definition does not derive them from the background field or from another framework.\n- The **kinetic four-momentum** is the derived vector $P:=mU$ along $z$, with unit kilogram metre per second. It is distinct from the potential-dependent covector defined in [[lem-rpm-charge-gauge-boundary]].\n- The **spin or polarization vector** is a $C^1$ tangent vector field $s(\\tau)\\in T_{z(\\tau)}M$ constrained by $g(s,U)=0$, so it lies in the positive-definite instantaneous rest space $U^\\perp$. Its unit is angular momentum, kilogram metre squared per second. This is a classical model vector; no quantization or quantum spin state is defined here.\n- The gyromagnetic ratio $g_s\\in\\mathbb R$ is a dimensionless primitive species parameter. In the instantaneous rest space the model defines the magnetic-moment vector by $\\mu=(g_s q/2m)s$, with unit ampere metre squared. No value $g_s=2$ is assumed, and this constitutive relation is not deduced from Lorentz covariance or classical electromagnetism.\n- The prescribed external electromagnetic field is a $C^1$ alternating covariant two-tensor $F$ on an open region of $M$, independent of the unknown curve. Define $F^\\sharp:T M\\to T M$ by $g(F^\\sharp(v),w)=F(w,v)$. In the flat specialization, a named inertial chart with $x^0=ct$ and $x^i$ in metres uses $F_{0i}=-E_i/c$ and $F_{ij}=\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$, matching [[def-em-electromagnetic-tensor]]. On the curved background, $F$ is only a two-form; at an event its components in a chosen orthonormal tetrad use the same algebraic convention. Such a tetrad is a basis field, not a coordinate chart or an observer congruence, as distinguished in [[def-rpm-spacetime-observer-and-worldline]]. The components in an orthonormal tetrad have tesla units. This is an explicit curved-background model input. The cited EM supplier is a flat Minkowski definition and does not derive a curved field or curved Maxwell system.\n- On any open potential domain where a $C^2$ covector field $A$ exists, $F=dA$ with components $F_{ab}=\\partial_aA_b-\\partial_bA_a$ and $A$ has tesla metre components in the stated coordinates. No global potential is assumed, and no Maxwell equation or constitutive law is imposed here.\n\nThis is a test-body model on a fixed background: self-gravity, gravitational backreaction, self-field evaluation, radiation reaction, collisions, electric dipole moments, field-gradient translational forces and higher multipoles are outside this definition. Units follow the framework-local SI conventions of [[def-em-si-conventions]] and do not identify quantities across frameworks.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "post-rpm-external-charge-action",
          "source_section": "Remark",
          "quote": "For dependent deductions, the adopted premise is that on a finite fixed-endpoint timelike segment in a local potential domain, the model trajectory is stationary for the displayed action. The potential and metric are fixed inputs, $m>0$ and $q$ are constant, and this premise asserts neither a global potential nor global trajectory existence.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A2",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A3",
          "source": "post-sr-massive-particle-dynamics",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Adopt as physical assumptions of the relativistic particle model, on top of the\nflat spacetime model of [[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]] and the SI unit\nconventions of [[def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions]], together with\nthe framework's idealisations of point particles, ideal clocks and ideal light\nsignals:\n\n**(i) Mass and momentum.** Every massive classical particle of the model\ncarries a positive constant invariant mass parameter $m>0$ and has\nfour-momentum $P=mU$, where $U$ is the four-velocity of its future timelike\nworldline ([[def-sr-regular-causal-curve-and-worldline]],\n[[def-sr-four-velocity-and-four-acceleration]],\n[[thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition]]); $P$ is a vector of $V$\nwhose SI unit is kilogram metre per second. The formula is adopted only for\n$m>0$; the case $m=0$ is not defined by it and is the separate premise\n[[post-sr-ideal-massless-particle-momentum]]. In particular $\\eta(P,P)=-m^2c^2$\nis a consequence of the four-velocity normalisation, not a separate\nassumption.\n\n**(ii) Observer measurement identification.** Relative to a future unit\nobserver $n$, the model identifies the measured energy of the particle with\n$E_n(P)$, its measured spatial momentum with $q_n(P)$, its kinetic energy with\n$T_n(P)=E_n(P)-mc^2$ and its rest energy with $mc^2$, where $E_n,q_n,T_n$ are\nthe quantities of [[def-sr-observed-energy-momentum-and-kinetic-energy]]; the\nunits are the joule, kilogram metre per second and kilogram of\n[[def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions]]. This clause is a formulation\nconvention identifying mathematical quantities with the quantities the model\ncalls measured; it is not a measurement report and no experiment is claimed.\n\n**(iii) Isolated collision balance.** An isolated collision is represented by\na mass-specified on-shell collision datum at an event, with finite nonempty\nincoming and outgoing lists of the massive particles of (i) and/or the ideal\nmassless particles of [[post-sr-ideal-massless-particle-momentum]], and with\nno external four-momentum transfer or omitted leg. The interaction is\nidealized as confined to that event. The sum of the incoming four-momenta equals the sum of the\noutgoing four-momenta, in the sense of the collision data of\n[[def-sr-collision-datum-and-conservation]]: the model adopts\n$Q_{\\mathrm{in}}=Q_{\\mathrm{out}}$ for such an isolated datum. No particular\ncollision is asserted to occur, and no mechanism, probability, cross section\nor amplitude is supplied by this clause.\n\n**(iv) Equation of motion for a stipulated force.** Put\n$H_c^+:=\\{U\\in C^+:\\eta(U,U)=-c^2\\}$. A stipulated force law is a given\nmap $F:D\\to V$ on a given subset $D\\subseteq A\\times H_c^+$,\nwith force components in $\\mathrm{kg}\\,\\mathrm m/\\mathrm s^2$. No regularity\naway from the specified worldline is assumed. Along a given $C^2$ massive worldline\n$x:I\\to A$ with $(x(s),U(s))\\in D$ for all $s\\in I$, the model's particle\nequation is $m\\,dU/d\\tau=F(x,U)$, with\n$dU/d\\tau$ the proper-time derivative of\n[[def-sr-proper-time-derivative-and-four-force]], so that the stipulated $F$\nis the four-force of the motion. A particular force law, and the existence,\nuniqueness and domain of solutions of the resulting equation, are not adopted\nhere and belong to a separate model.\n\nEmpirical support for these assumptions is a nonlogical relation of the\nframework, not a proof; the idealisations of point particles, ideal clocks,\nideal light signals and flat spacetime are inherited and travel to every\nconsumer of this premise.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "pthm-rpm-charge-mass-shell",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the charged test-body quantities of [[def-rpm-charge-spin-model]], the field convention of [[def-em-electromagnetic-tensor]], and the stationary-action equivalence [[pthm-rpm-external-charge-action-equivalence]], and assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ for the canonical tangent-bundle smooth structure. For every future-timelike initial datum $(x_0,U_0)$ with $g(U_0,U_0)=-c^2$, the equation of motion has a unique local solution by [[lem-rpm-external-charge-local-ivp]]. Along it the kinetic four-momentum $P=mU$ remains future timelike and satisfies\n\n$$g(P,P)=-m^2c^2.$$\n\nIn the flat Minkowski specialization, use the massive-particle momentum result [[pthm-sr-momentum-and-four-force]] under its stated model assumptions, and choose a named inertial chart with $x^0=ct$, $E,B$ defined by [[def-em-electromagnetic-tensor]], and $\\mathbf v$ the Euclidean spatial chart velocity. Set $\\gamma:=(1-|\\mathbf v|^2/c^2)^{-1/2}$. Then, with $\\mathbf p=m\\gamma\\mathbf v$ and $E_{\\rm part}=\\gamma mc^2$ supplied by [[pthm-sr-momentum-and-four-force]], the same equation gives\n\n$$\\frac{d\\mathbf p}{dt}=q(\\mathbf E+\\mathbf v\\times\\mathbf B),\\qquad \\frac{dE_{\\rm part}}{dt}=q\\,\\mathbf E\\cdot\\mathbf v.$$\n\nThe mass-shell and force statements are conditional deductions within the specified model; they do not establish empirical applicability.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-em-electromagnetic-tensor",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let the laboratory region, its inertial chart and the fields and sources be those\nof [[def-em-lab-geometry]], [[def-em-fields]] and [[def-em-sources]], with the\nunits of [[def-em-si-conventions]]; fix the ordered basis\n$(e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$ of the chart and the oriented volume form $\\varepsilon$ of\n[[def-oriented-minkowski-volume-form]], with $\\varepsilon_{0123}=+1$ and\n$\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$.\n\n(a) [Components] For a field pair $(E,B)$ of class $C^k$, $k\\ge1$, define the\ncovariant components of the **electromagnetic two-form** $F$ in the chart by\n$F_{00}=0$, $F_{0i}:=-E_i/c$, $F_{ij}:=\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$\n($i,j,k\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$) and $F_{ab}:=-F_{ba}$, the index-raised components being\n$F^{0i}=E_i/c$, $F^{i0}=-E_i/c$ and $F^{ij}=\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$. Here\n$\\epsilon_{ijk}$ is the spatial permutation symbol of\n[[def-oriented-minkowski-volume-form]]. Then $F$ is a $C^k$ type-$(0,2)$\ntensor field on $U$ in the sense of\n[[def-ck-tensor-field-on-minkowski-space]], alternating at every event, and the\ncomponents above are exactly its component functions: at $p\\in U$ the tensor\n$F(p)$ is the bilinear form $F(p)(u,w)=F_{ab}(p)u^aw^b$ on $V$ in the\ncoordinates $u^a,w^b$ of $u,w$ in $(e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$. Indeed each $F_{ab}$ is\na finite sum of products of the $C^k$ functions $E_i,B_k$ of the chart\ncoordinates with the constants $c,\\epsilon_{ijk}$, hence a $C^k$ function of\n$x(p)$, and the component criterion of\n[[def-ck-tensor-field-on-minkowski-space]] applies.\n\n(b) [Observer decomposition] Let $n:=e_0$ be the future unit timelike vector of\nthe chart and let $e_a:=F_{ab}n^b$, $b_a:=(*F)_{ab}n^b$ be the observer\ncomponents of [[thm-sr-antisymmetric-observer-decomposition]]; then\n$e_i=+E_i/c$, $b_i=-B_i$ and $e_0=b_0=0$, and the invariants and square wedge\nof that theorem read\n\n$$\\tfrac12F_{ab}F^{ab}=|B|^2-|E|^2/c^2,\\qquad F_{ab}(*F)^{ab}=+4\\,(E\\cdot B)/c,\\qquad F\\wedge F=-2\\,(E\\cdot B)\\,\\varepsilon/c,$$\n\nwhere $|\\cdot|$ and $\\cdot$ are the spatial Euclidean structure of the frame.\n(For the same pair $(E,B)$, that theorem's optional clause (d) uses\n$F_{\\rm optional}^{i0}=E_i/c$ and $F_{\\rm optional}^{ij}=-\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$, so\n$F_{\\rm optional}=-F$ in all six components, not only the magnetic block. This\nframework fixes clause (a) and uses the theorem's general algebra; its optional\nSI dictionary must be converted by negating the entire tensor.)\n\n(c) [Potentials when they exist] If $\\varphi$ and $A$ are $C^1$ with\n$E=-\\nabla\\varphi-\\partial_tA$ and $B=\\operatorname{curl}A$, and\n$A^\\mu:=(\\varphi/c,A)$, $A_\\mu:=\\eta_{\\mu\\nu}A^\\nu$, then\n$F_{\\mu\\nu}=\\partial_\\mu A_\\nu-\\partial_\\nu A_\\mu$; conversely, if a $C^1$\ncovector field $A_\\mu$ on $U$ satisfies $F_{\\mu\\nu}=\\partial_\\mu A_\\nu\n-\\partial_\\nu A_\\mu$, then $E,B$ recovered from $F$ by clause (a) obey\n$E=-\\nabla\\varphi-\\partial_tA$ and $B=\\operatorname{curl}A$ with\n$\\varphi:=-cA_0$ and the spatial potential vector $A:=(A_1,A_2,A_3)$. Here\n$\\nabla$ and $\\operatorname{curl}$ are those of\n[[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]] in the spatial coordinates,\nand the spatial cross-product conventions of [[def-cross-product-in-r3]] are\nused. The definition does not assert that every field pair admits such a\npotential; where a $C^1$ potential represents the $C^1$-or-better field of\nclause (a), the field is closed in the explicit sense\n$\\partial_\\lambda F_{\\mu\\nu}+\\partial_\\mu F_{\\nu\\lambda}\n+\\partial_\\nu F_{\\lambda\\mu}=0$. This conclusion does not require second\nclassical derivatives of the potential: put\n$H_{\\lambda\\mu\\nu}:=\\partial_\\lambda F_{\\mu\\nu}+\\partial_\\mu F_{\\nu\\lambda}\n+\\partial_\\nu F_{\\lambda\\mu}$, which is continuous because $F$ is $C^1$; for\nevery smooth compactly supported test $\\psi$, substituting $F=dA$ and\nintegrating by parts twice, moving the derivatives first onto $\\psi$\n([[lem-sr-compact-variation-and-fundamental-lemma]] (b)) and then off the\npotential factors after commuting only the smooth test derivatives\n([[thm-clairaut-schwarz-mixed-partials]]), gives $\\int H\\psi=0$; the\nfundamental lemma (a) of [[lem-sr-compact-variation-and-fundamental-lemma]]\nthen gives $H=0$. The framework's potential theorems establish existence only\nunder their stated hypotheses; [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]]\nsupplies the coordinate derivatives $\\partial_\\mu$ used throughout.\n\n(d) [Four-current and index-raising operator] With the adopted four-current\n$j=(c\\rho,J)$ of [[def-em-sources]], the raised-index operator $\\Phi$ of\n[[lem-em-antisymmetric-tensor-contraction]], defined pointwise on $V$ by\n$\\eta(\\Phi(u),w)=F(w,u)$, satisfies\n$\\Phi(u)^\\mu=\\eta^{\\mu\\alpha}F_{\\alpha\\beta}u^\\beta$; the components of $F$\ncarry the tesla (T), those of $j$ carry A m$^{-2}$, and those of a\nfour-potential $A^\\mu$ carry T m.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-em-si-conventions",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Adopt the SI system of units for this framework, with the following quantity\ntable and conventions; every symbol is a quantity of this framework and the\nlaboratory structure is that of [[def-em-lab-geometry]].\n\n(a) [Coordinates and constants] $x^0=ct$ and each spatial coordinate $x^i$\ncarries the metre (m); the time coordinate $t$ carries the second (s); the\nconversion constant $c>0$ carries m s$^{-1}$ and has the exact SI defining\nvalue $c=299\\,792\\,458\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$; the elementary charge $e$ has the\nexact SI defining value $e=1.602\\,176\\,634\\times10^{-19}\\ \\mathrm{C}$, and this\nitem does not assert that physical charge is quantised; the magnetic constant\n$\\mu_0>0$ carries N A$^{-2}$ and is measured, so no exact numerical value of\n$\\mu_0$ is asserted. The pre-2019 SI fixed the historical nominal\n$\\mu_{0,\\mathrm{nom}}:=4\\pi\\times10^{-7}\\ \\mathrm{H\\,m^{-1}}\n=4\\pi\\times10^{-7}\\ \\mathrm{N\\,A^{-2}}$ exactly; since the revised SI took\neffect on 20 May 2019, $\\mu_0$ is experimentally determined. The SI brochure\nquotes the nominal $\\mu_{0,\\mathrm{nom}}$ as the estimate at the 2019 adoption\nwith relative standard uncertainty $2.3\\times10^{-10}$; this is historical\nestimate and uncertainty context, not an exact equality for the actual $\\mu_0$\nor a current measured value. The electric constant is defined within the\nframework by\n\n$$\\varepsilon_0:=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$$\n\nin F m$^{-1}$, consistent with the exact SI relation $\\varepsilon_0\\mu_0=1/c^2$,\nso that $c^2\\varepsilon_0\\mu_0=1$ and $\\varepsilon_0$ inherits the relative\nstandard uncertainty of $\\mu_0$.\n\n(b) [Electromagnetic quantities] Charge $q$ carries the coulomb (C); the\ncharge density $\\rho$ of the framework's source definition carries C m$^{-3}$\nand the current density $J$ carries A m$^{-2}$, so that the four-current\ncomponents $(c\\rho,J)$ carry A m$^{-2}$; the electric field $E$ carries\nV m$^{-1}$; the magnetic field $B$ carries the tesla (T), where\n$1\\ \\mathrm{T}=1\\ \\mathrm{N\\,A^{-1}\\,m^{-1}}$; the scalar potential $\\varphi$\ncarries V and the vector potential $A$ carries T m, so that the four-potential\ncomponents $(\\varphi/c,A)$ carry T m; the components of the electromagnetic\ntwo-form of the framework's tensor definition carry T; a force carries the\nnewton (N), $1\\ \\mathrm{N}=1\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-2}}$.\n\n(c) [Mechanical quantities] A rest mass parameter $m$ carries the kilogram\n(kg) and a four-momentum $P=mU$ of the relativistic particle model carries\nkg m s$^{-1}$; field energy density carries J m$^{-3}$, energy flux\nW m$^{-2}$, so that $1\\ \\mathrm{W}=1\\ \\mathrm{J\\,s^{-1}}=1\\ \\mathrm{V\\,A}$.\n\n(d) [Bookkeeping only] The unit of a quantity records the adopted system of\nunits; it does not show that a quantity is primitive in this or any other\nframework, it contains no measurement, and it does not identify a quantity\nwith a similarly named quantity of another framework. The exact defining\nvalues of $c$ and $e$ fix numerical conventions of the SI; they do not by\nthemselves identify $c$ with the vacuum propagation constant of ideal light,\nwhich is the physical adoption of the framework's Minkowski postulate.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-rpm-relativistic-coulomb-phase-system",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $m,c,\\kappa>0$, let $x,p\\in\\mathbb R^3$, and set\n\n$$D:=(\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{0\\})\\times\\mathbb R^3,\\qquad r:=|x|,\\qquad \\mathcal E(p):=\\sqrt{m^2c^4+c^2|p|^2}.$$\n\nThe **repulsive relativistic Coulomb Hamiltonian** is\n\n$$H(x,p):=\\mathcal E(p)+\\frac{\\kappa}{r},$$\n\nand its **phase system** is the autonomous ODE on the open set $D$\n\n$$\\dot x=\\nabla_pH=\\frac{c^2p}{\\mathcal E(p)},\\qquad \\dot p=-\\nabla_xH=\\frac{\\kappa x}{r^3}.$$\n\nHere the system parameter $t$ is time in seconds and dot denotes $d/dt$; $m$ is a positive mass parameter (kilograms in the SI interpretation), $c$ a positive speed parameter (metres per second), and $\\kappa$ a positive energy-times-length parameter (joule-metres). Define the radial momentum $p_r:=x\\cdot p/r$ and angular-momentum vector $J:=x\\times p$. A trajectory is a $C^1$ solution of this ODE on an interval; maximality is in the sense of [[def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution]].\n\nThis is only a mathematical phase system. Its physical interpretation as a charged particle in an externally prescribed Coulomb field requires separate model assumptions.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "prop-rpm-relativistic-coulomb-scattering-branch",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Consider the phase system of [[def-rpm-relativistic-coulomb-phase-system]] with $m,c,\\kappa>0$. Let a trajectory have conserved energy $W>mc^2$ and angular-momentum magnitude $J:=|x\\times p|>\\kappa/c$. Define\n\n$$A:=\\frac{W^2-m^2c^4}{c^2}>0,\\qquad d:=\\frac{W\\kappa}{c^2}>0,\\qquad C:=J^2-\\frac{\\kappa^2}{c^2}>0.$$\n\nThen its squared radial momentum is\n\n$$p_r^2=Q(u):=A-2du-Cu^2,\\qquad u:=1/r.$$\n\nThere is a unique positive simple root\n\n$$u_*:=\\frac{\\sqrt{d^2+CA}-d}{C},\\qquad 0<u_*<\\frac{W-mc^2}{\\kappa}.$$\n\nThe trajectory through the turning data $r_*=1/u_*$, $p_r=0$, and $|x\\times p|=J$ is global for all system times, has one noncollision periapsis, and is asymptotically incoming and outgoing. Its impact parameter, defined as the limiting perpendicular distance of either asymptote from the force center, is\n\n$$b=\\frac{J}{\\sqrt A}>0.$$\n\nIf $\\Delta$ is the positive azimuthal change from either asymptote to periapsis, then\n\n$$\\Delta=\\frac{J}{\\sqrt C}\\arctan\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{CA}}{d}\\right),\\qquad \\theta:=\\pi-2\\Delta\\in(0,\\pi),$$\n\nwhere $\\theta$ is the angle between the incoming and outgoing asymptotic velocity vectors. This formula is for the repulsive branch $J>\\kappa/c$; it does not cover $J\\le\\kappa/c$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "In the inertial chart, $dx^0/dt=c$ and $|d\\mathbf x/dt|=|\\mathbf v|$. The action density from [A1] is therefore $L(\\mathbf x,\\mathbf v)=-mc^2\\sqrt{1-|\\mathbf v|^2/c^2}+qA_0c=-mc^2\\sqrt{1-|\\mathbf v|^2/c^2}-q\\phi(r).$ Its spatial canonical momentum is $\\partial L/\\partial\\mathbf v=m\\gamma\\mathbf v$; because $A_i=0$, it is also the kinetic momentum in [F1]. The Legendre expression $\\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf v-L$ equals $\\gamma mc^2+q\\phi=\\sqrt{m^2c^4+c^2|\\mathbf p|^2}+qQ/(4\\pi\\varepsilon_0r)$. With $\\kappa=qQ/(4\\pi\\varepsilon_0)>0$, this is precisely the Hamiltonian in [F3].",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "A2",
            "A3",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra",
            "F3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "From [F2], $\\mathbf E=-\\nabla\\phi=Q\\mathbf x/(4\\pi\\varepsilon_0r^3)$. Since $\\mathbf B=0$, [F1] gives $\\dot{\\mathbf p}=q\\mathbf E=\\kappa\\mathbf x/r^3$. The Hamiltonian derivative gives $\\dot{\\mathbf x}=c^2\\mathbf p/\\sqrt{m^2c^4+c^2|\\mathbf p|^2}$. These are exactly the two phase equations of [F3], so each local physical solution in the prescribed model is a trajectory of that mathematical system.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "A2",
            "A3",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "For $W>mc^2$ and $J>\\kappa/c$, all hypotheses of the exact repulsive scattering proposition [F3] hold. It supplies the unique outer turning branch and the stated impact parameter and deflection formula, with $A,d,C$ as defined in the statement. The conclusion is conditional on [A1], [A2], and [A3] and the fixed-source idealization.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1",
            "A1",
            "A2",
            "A3",
            "F3",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "The exact angle is therefore a model prediction for the stated branch. The derivation makes no claim for $J\\le\\kappa/c$, for source recoil or radiation, or for any experimental result.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 3.1",
            "A1",
            "A2",
            "A3",
            "given"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
