    "ex-rpm-schwarzschild-null-critical-impact": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-rpm-schwarzschild-null-geodesic-reduction",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(M,g,N)$ be the Schwarzschild exterior datum with $s>0$ and $f(r)=1-s/r$ on $r>s$. Let $\\gamma(\\lambda)$ be an affinely parametrized null geodesic of its metric-selected connection whose image lies in the equatorial plane $\\vartheta=\\pi/2$, and choose the orientation so that\n\n$$E:=f(r)\\frac{dx^0}{d\\lambda}>0,\\qquad L:=r^2\\frac{d\\varphi}{d\\lambda}\\ge0.$$\n\nBoth quantities are constant along $\\gamma$. With impact parameter $b:=L/E\\ge0$ and $H(r):=f(r)/r^2$, its radial equation is\n\n$$\\left(\\frac{dr}{d\\lambda}\\right)^2=E^2\\bigl(1-b^2H(r)\\bigr).$$\n\nMoreover, $H'(r)=(3s-2r)/r^4$, so $H$ has its unique exterior maximum at $r_{\\rm ph}=3s/2$, with $H(r_{\\rm ph})=4/(27s^2)$. Thus the critical impact parameter for a radial turning point is $b_{\\rm c}=3\\sqrt3\\,s/2$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "pthm-rpm-schwarzschild-null-scattering",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Adopt the Schwarzschild exterior vacuum model with $s>0$ and the ideal-light rule that rays are future null geodesics. For an equatorial incoming ray with constants $E>0$, $L\\ge0$, impact parameter $b=L/E$, and\n\n$$H(r)=\\frac{1-s/r}{r^2},\\qquad b_{\\rm c}=\\frac{3\\sqrt3}{2}s,\\qquad r_{\\rm ph}=\\frac32s,$$\n\nthe radial first integral is $\\dot r^2=E^2[1-b^2H(r)]$ as in [[lem-rpm-schwarzschild-null-geodesic-reduction]], where dot denotes affine-parameter derivative. Then:\n\n1. If $b>b_{\\rm c}$, there is a unique outer turning point $r_*(b)>r_{\\rm ph}$, which is reached in finite affine parameter; the ray from infinity turns and returns to infinity. Its total azimuthal advance is\n\n   $$\\Delta\\varphi=2\\int_{r_*}^{\\infty}\\frac{b\\,dr}{r^2\\sqrt{1-b^2H(r)}}.$$\n\n   This integral converges at both endpoints.\n2. If $b=b_{\\rm c}$, the incoming branch from infinity approaches $r_{\\rm ph}$ only as affine parameter tends to $+\\infty$; its azimuthal advance diverges logarithmically.\n3. If $0\\le b<b_{\\rm c}$, the inward branch has no exterior turning point and reaches the boundary $r=s$ of the Schwarzschild exterior in finite affine parameter. Only if the ray is continued in the specified Kruskal model, once it lies in the region $U\\ge0$, does the causal escape result imply that it cannot return to the right exterior.\n\nThese are geodesic-model deductions. The impact threshold is not by itself an observable shadow radius.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Since $(2\\sqrt2s)^2=8s^2$ and $H(2s)=1/(8s^2)$, the radial factor vanishes at $2s$. Also $2\\sqrt2s>3\\sqrt3s/2$ because $32>27$, and $H$ is strictly decreasing above $3s/2$, so this is the unique outer root. The turning and return classification follows from [F2].",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "Substituting $b_c^2=27s^2/4$ and $H=(r-s)/r^3$ and expanding the numerator verifies the displayed factorization. The double zero at $r=3s/2$ and the theorem's affine and angular quadratures then give the critical asymptote and logarithmic divergence.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "These are exact ideal-geodesic conclusions in the Schwarzschild model; no wave-optics or observational statement is added.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "given"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
