    "ex-rpm-schwarzschild-static-redshift-acceleration": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "pthm-rpm-static-clock-redshift",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $s=2G_Nm_*/c^2>0$, $f(r)=1-s/r$, and let $r_E,r_R>s$ be the emitter and receiver radii of static observers in the adopted Schwarzschild exterior model. With $n=f^{-1/2}\\partial_0$ the unit static congruence and $U=cn$ the corresponding four-velocity, the ideal-clock and geometric-optics predictions are\n\n$$\\frac{\\Delta\\tau_R}{\\Delta\\tau_E}=\\frac{\\omega_E}{\\omega_R}=\\sqrt{\\frac{f(r_R)}{f(r_E)}},\\qquad \\frac{\\omega_R}{\\omega_E}=\\sqrt{\\frac{f(r_E)}{f(r_R)}}.$$\n\nThe proper four-acceleration magnitude required to remain static at radius $r>s$ is\n\n$$a(r)=\\sqrt{g(\\nabla_UU,\\nabla_UU)}=\\frac{c^2s}{2r^2\\sqrt{f(r)}}=\\frac{G_Nm_*}{r^2\\sqrt{1-s/r}}.$$\n\nFor any $0\\le\\rho<1$ and radii satisfying $0<s/r_E,s/r_R\\le\\rho$, define $x_i=s/r_i$ and write $\\log:=L$ for the natural integral logarithm of [[def-integral-logarithm]]. The exact logarithmic ratio has the uniform expansion\n\n$$\\log\\frac{\\omega_R}{\\omega_E}=\\frac{s}{2}\\left(\\frac1{r_R}-\\frac1{r_E}\\right)+R,\\qquad |R|\\le\\frac{x_E^2+x_R^2}{4(1-\\rho)}.$$\n\nThese are conditional deductions of the idealized model; no measured redshift or acceleration is asserted.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-gr-schwarzschild-curvature-and-kretschmann-scalar",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(M,g,N)$ be the Schwarzschild exterior datum of mass parameter $m_*$,\nSchwarzschild radius $s=2G_Nm_*/c^2$ and $f(r)=1-s/r$\n([[def-gr-schwarzschild-exterior-model]]), with curvature data as in\n[[lem-gr-schwarzschild-ricci-and-einstein-tensors]]. On each spherical-polar\nproduct chart $(x^0,r,\\vartheta,\\varphi)$ define the local frame field\n\n$$e_{\\hat0}:=f^{-1/2}\\partial_0,\\qquad e_{\\hat1}:=f^{1/2}\\partial_1,\\qquad e_{\\hat2}:=r^{-1}\\partial_2,\\qquad e_{\\hat3}:=(r\\sin\\vartheta)^{-1}\\partial_3,$$\n\nand for the curvature tensor define the **frame components**\n\n$$\\hat R_{\\hat a\\hat b\\hat c\\hat d}:=g\\bigl(R(e_{\\hat c},e_{\\hat d})e_{\\hat b},e_{\\hat a}\\bigr).$$\n\nThen:\n\n**(a) The frame field.** $(e_{\\hat0},e_{\\hat1},e_{\\hat2},e_{\\hat3})$ is a\nsmooth $g$-orthonormal frame (tetrad field) on the chart domain, with\n$g(e_{\\hat a},e_{\\hat b})=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)_{\\hat a\\hat b}$, and\n$e_{\\hat0}$ is future-directed timelike; it is a tetrad field in the sense of\n[[def-gr-local-frame-and-tetrad-fields]]. It is not a coordinate frame.\n\n**(b) Frame components.** The nonzero frame components, up to the\nsymmetries $R_{abcd}=-R_{bacd}=-R_{abdc}=R_{cdab}$ of\n[[thm-gr-curvature-algebraic-symmetries]], are\n\n$$\\hat R_{\\hat0\\hat1\\hat0\\hat1}=\\frac{f''}{2},\\qquad \\hat R_{\\hat0\\hat2\\hat0\\hat2}=\\hat R_{\\hat0\\hat3\\hat0\\hat3}=\\frac{f'}{2r},$$\n\n$$\\hat R_{\\hat1\\hat2\\hat1\\hat2}=\\hat R_{\\hat1\\hat3\\hat1\\hat3}=-\\frac{f'}{2r},\\qquad \\hat R_{\\hat2\\hat3\\hat2\\hat3}=\\frac{1-f}{r^2},$$\n\nwith $f'=s/r^2$ and $f''=-2s/r^3$; all other frame components vanish.\n\n**(c) Kretschmann scalar.** Define the global smooth scalar field\n$K:=R_{abcd}R^{abcd}$, with indices raised by the inverse metric. On the\nspherical-polar chart locus its component expression is\n\n$$K=f''^2+\\frac{4(f')^2}{r^2}+\\frac{4(1-f)^2}{r^4},$$\n\nand for the model function $f=1-s/r$ this is\n\n$$K=\\frac{12s^2}{r^6}=\\frac{48\\,G_N^2m_*^2}{c^4\\,r^6}.$$\n\nThe scalar identity is first computed on the regular angular locus and holds\non all of $M$ by smoothness and density. Since $r>s$ on $M$, one has\n$0<K=12s^2/r^6<12/s^4$ at every point; as $r\\downarrow s$ the expression has\nthe finite boundary value $12/s^4$, and the expression $12s^2/r^6$, read as a\nfunction of $r>0$, grows without bound as $r\\downarrow0$.\n\n**(d) Scope.** The lemma computes curvature data of the exterior datum on\n$r>s$. The limit $r\\downarrow s$ is the boundary value on $M$ of the smooth\nexpression $12s^2/r^6$; no value of the metric at $r=s$ or at $r=0$ is used\nor asserted here, those regions being outside the datum (the regular\ncontinuation across $r=s$ is the separate extension lemma of this page).",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-gr-schwarzschild-extension-chart",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(M,g,N)$ be the Schwarzschild exterior datum with Schwarzschild radius\n$s>0$ and $f(r)=1-s/r$\n([[def-gr-schwarzschild-exterior-model]]). Let\n$M_{\\mathrm{ext}}:=\\mathbb R\\times(0,\\infty)\\times S^2$ carry the product\nsmooth structure and let $\\iota_{\\mathrm{ext}}:M_{\\mathrm{ext}}\\to S^2$ be the\nthird projection. Its product atlas includes ordinary sphere charts at the\npoles ([[prop-products-of-smooth-manifolds-have-a-canonical-product-smooth-structure]]).\nDefine the **tortoise function**\n\n$$r_*(r):=r+s\\log\\left|\\frac{r}{s}-1\\right|,\\qquad r>0,\\ r\\ne s,$$\n\non each of the two intervals $(0,s)$ and $(s,\\infty)$, and define the\n**advanced-chart metric** globally on $M_{\\mathrm{ext}}$ by\n\n$$g_{\\mathrm{ext}}=-f(r)\\,dv\\otimes dv+dv\\otimes dr+dr\\otimes dv+r^2\\,\\iota_{\\mathrm{ext}}^*\\gamma_{S^2}.$$\n\nIn spherical-polar product charts its components are\n\n$$g_{\\mathrm{ext}}=-f(r)\\,dv^2+2\\,dv\\,dr+r^2\\,d\\Omega^2,$$\n\nnamely $g_{vv}=-f$, $g_{vr}=g_{rv}=1$,\n$g_{\\vartheta\\vartheta}=r^2$, $g_{\\varphi\\varphi}=r^2\\sin^2\\vartheta$ and all\nother components zero. In another sphere chart the angular block is\n$r^2(\\gamma_{ij})$. Then:\n\n**(a) Smooth Lorentzian metric.** $g_{\\mathrm{ext}}$ is a smooth symmetric\ncovariant $2$-tensor field on $M_{\\mathrm{ext}}$, and at every point it is\nnondegenerate with inertia $(3,1,0)$; hence $(M_{\\mathrm{ext}},g_{\\mathrm{ext}})$\nis a smooth Lorentzian four-manifold of signature $(-,+,+,+)$ on all of\n$r>0$, including $r=s$.\n\n**(b) Time orientation.** The smooth vector field\n\n$$N_{\\mathrm{ext}}:=A(r)\\,\\partial_v-\\partial_r,\\qquad A(r):=\\frac{f(r)+\\sqrt{f(r)^2+4}}{2}>0,$$\n\nsatisfies $g_{\\mathrm{ext}}(N_{\\mathrm{ext}},N_{\\mathrm{ext}})<0$ everywhere\nand $g_{\\mathrm{ext}}(N_{\\mathrm{ext}},\\partial_v)<0$ everywhere; hence\n$N_{\\mathrm{ext}}$ is a time orientation of $g_{\\mathrm{ext}}$, and on the\nregion $r>s$ it determines the same future cone as the datum's time\norientation $\\partial_v=\\partial_0$.\n\n**(c) Isometric embedding of the exterior.** The map\n$\\Phi:M\\to M_{\\mathrm{ext}}$, $(x^0,r,\\omega)\\mapsto(x^0+r_*(r),r,\\omega)$\nrestricts to a diffeomorphism of $M$ onto the open subset $\\{r>s\\}$ of\n$M_{\\mathrm{ext}}$, and $\\Phi^*g_{\\mathrm{ext}}=g$; the exterior datum is\ntherefore isometric to the region $r>s$ of the extension.\n\n**(d) Vacuum and Kretschmann scalar of the extension.** The\nmetric-selected connection of $g_{\\mathrm{ext}}$ exists and is unique, its\nRicci tensor, scalar curvature and Einstein tensor vanish identically on\n$M_{\\mathrm{ext}}$, and its Kretschmann scalar equals\n\n$$R_{abcd}R^{abcd}=\\frac{12s^2}{r^6}$$\n\non all of $M_{\\mathrm{ext}}$. In particular the scalar is smooth and finite on\nthe horizon $r=s$, where it takes the value $12/s^4$.\n\n**(e) The horizon is a null hypersurface.** The level set $\\{r=s\\}$ is a\nsmooth null hypersurface of $(M_{\\mathrm{ext}},g_{\\mathrm{ext}})$ whose null\ngenerator direction is $\\partial_v$; every smooth product-chart frame remains\na basis across $r=s$. In spherical-polar charts\n$\\det(g_{\\mathrm{ext}})=-r^4\\sin^2\\vartheta$; in an arbitrary sphere chart\nthe determinant is $-r^4\\det(\\gamma_{ij})$, which is nonzero.\n\n**(f) Scope.** The extension constructed here is a smooth Lorentzian\nmanifold containing the exterior isometrically and regular across $r=s$; it\nis not claimed to be maximal, and no uniqueness or global structure theorem\nis asserted. The region $r\\le0$ and the surfaces relevant to the Kruskal\nmodel of this page are outside this statement.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Substitution gives $f_E=1/2$ and $f_R=3/4$. Applying [F1] yields the frequency and clock ratios. The acceleration at $2s$ is $c^2s/[2(2s)^2\\sqrt{1/2}]=c^2/(4\\sqrt2s)$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "At $r=s(1+\\epsilon)$, $f=\\epsilon/(1+\\epsilon)$, so [F1] gives $a=c^2/[2s\\sqrt\\epsilon(1+\\epsilon)^{3/2}]$, which diverges as $\\epsilon\\downarrow0$. By [F2], $K=12s^2/[s^6(1+\\epsilon)^6]$ tends to $12/s^4$, a finite value.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "[F3] supplies a smooth extension through the same $r=s$ boundary. Hence the divergence in 1.2 is the static-observer acceleration requirement in the exterior chart, while this curvature scalar remains finite and the metric has the stated smooth extension. No measured clock ratio or acceleration is asserted.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "given"
          ]
        }
      ],
